WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.056 --> 00:02.236 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference December 15,

00:02.236 --> 00:03.326 align:middle
2021 CHAIR POWELL.

00:03.326 --> 00:03.636 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:03.636 --> 00:05.786 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:05.786 --> 00:08.006 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:08.006 --> 00:09.196 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

00:09.666 --> 00:11.536 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

00:12.416 --> 00:14.616 align:middle
Today, in support
of these goals,

00:14.686 --> 00:17.296 align:middle
the Federal Open Market
Committee kept interest rates

00:17.296 --> 00:20.616 align:middle
near zero and updated its
assessment of the progress

00:20.616 --> 00:24.396 align:middle
that the economy has made
toward the criteria specified

00:24.396 --> 00:26.806 align:middle
in the Committee's forward
guidance for interest rates.

00:27.336 --> 00:30.726 align:middle
In addition, in light of the
strengthening labor market

00:30.726 --> 00:34.166 align:middle
and elevated inflation
pressures, we decided to speed

00:34.166 --> 00:36.626 align:middle
up the reductions in
our asset purchases.

00:37.536 --> 00:40.886 align:middle
As I will explain, economic
developments and changes

00:40.886 --> 00:44.166 align:middle
in the outlook warrant this
evolution of monetary policy,

00:44.896 --> 00:47.306 align:middle
which will continue to
provide appropriate support

00:47.406 --> 00:48.196 align:middle
for the economy.

00:48.606 --> 00:52.726 align:middle
Economic activity is
on track to expand

00:52.726 --> 00:55.876 align:middle
at a robust pace this
year, reflecting progress

00:55.876 --> 00:58.556 align:middle
on vaccinations and the
reopening of the economy.

00:59.936 --> 01:04.016 align:middle
Aggregate demand remains
very strong, buoyed by fiscal

01:04.016 --> 01:07.366 align:middle
and monetary policy support and
the healthy financial positions

01:07.366 --> 01:08.976 align:middle
of households and businesses.

01:10.336 --> 01:12.516 align:middle
The rise in COVID
cases in recent weeks,

01:12.946 --> 01:15.486 align:middle
along with the emergence
of the Omicron variant,

01:16.246 --> 01:18.796 align:middle
pose[s] risks to the outlook.

01:20.176 --> 01:22.196 align:middle
Notwithstanding the
effects of the virus

01:22.196 --> 01:25.226 align:middle
and supply constraints,
FOMC participants continue

01:25.226 --> 01:27.106 align:middle
to foresee rapid growth.

01:28.096 --> 01:31.516 align:middle
As shown in our Summary of
Economic Projections (SEP),

01:31.516 --> 01:34.246 align:middle
the median projection for
real GDP growth stands

01:34.246 --> 01:37.786 align:middle
at 5.5 percent this year
and 4 percent next year.

01:38.306 --> 01:41.526 align:middle
Amid improving labor
market conditions

01:41.526 --> 01:43.276 align:middle
and very strong demand
for workers,

01:43.776 --> 01:46.206 align:middle
the economy has been
making rapid progress toward

01:46.206 --> 01:47.256 align:middle
maximum employment.

01:48.356 --> 01:50.686 align:middle
Job gains have been
solid in recent months,

01:50.736 --> 01:54.856 align:middle
averaging 378,000 per month
over the last three months.

01:56.256 --> 01:59.136 align:middle
The unemployment rate has
declined substantially-falling

01:59.136 --> 02:02.106 align:middle
6/10 of a percentage point
since our last meeting

02:02.636 --> 02:05.136 align:middle
and reaching 4.2
percent in November.

02:05.656 --> 02:09.386 align:middle
The recent improvements in labor
market conditions have narrowed

02:09.386 --> 02:12.246 align:middle
the differences in
employment across groups,

02:12.626 --> 02:14.476 align:middle
especially for workers
at the lower end

02:14.476 --> 02:15.596 align:middle
of the wage distribution,

02:16.206 --> 02:18.746 align:middle
as well as for African
Americans and Hispanics.

02:20.066 --> 02:22.576 align:middle
Labor force participation
showed a welcome rise

02:22.576 --> 02:27.176 align:middle
in November-but remains subdued,
in part reflecting the aging

02:27.176 --> 02:29.076 align:middle
of the population
and retirements.

02:30.416 --> 02:33.466 align:middle
In addition, some who otherwise
would be seeking work report

02:33.546 --> 02:36.526 align:middle
that they are out of the labor
force because of factors related

02:36.526 --> 02:39.626 align:middle
to the pandemic,
including caregiving needs

02:39.776 --> 02:41.896 align:middle
and ongoing concerns
about the virus.

02:43.226 --> 02:46.866 align:middle
At the same time, employers are
having difficulties filling job

02:46.866 --> 02:49.306 align:middle
openings, and wages are rising

02:49.306 --> 02:51.686 align:middle
at their fastest
pace in many years.

02:51.716 --> 02:56.786 align:middle
How long the labor shortages
will persist is unclear,

02:56.946 --> 02:59.726 align:middle
particularly if additional
waves of the virus occur.

03:01.076 --> 03:04.436 align:middle
Looking ahead, FOMC participants
project the labor market

03:04.476 --> 03:07.816 align:middle
to continue to improve,
with the median projection

03:07.816 --> 03:10.996 align:middle
for the unemployment rate
declining to 3.5 percent

03:10.996 --> 03:11.916 align:middle
by the end of the year.

03:13.426 --> 03:15.486 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in September,

03:15.706 --> 03:18.936 align:middle
participants have revised their
unemployment rate projections

03:18.936 --> 03:21.826 align:middle
noticeably lower for
this year and next.

03:23.286 --> 03:26.126 align:middle
Supply and demand imbalances
related to the pandemic

03:26.126 --> 03:28.136 align:middle
and [to] the reopening of
the economy have continued

03:28.136 --> 03:30.366 align:middle
to contribute to elevated
levels of inflation.

03:31.276 --> 03:32.766 align:middle
In particular, bottlenecks

03:32.766 --> 03:35.526 align:middle
and supply constraints are
limiting how quickly production

03:35.526 --> 03:38.046 align:middle
can respond to higher
demand in the near term.

03:39.096 --> 03:42.246 align:middle
These problems have been
larger and longer lasting

03:42.246 --> 03:45.956 align:middle
than anticipated, exacerbated
by waves of the virus.

03:46.786 --> 03:49.506 align:middle
As a result, overall
inflation is running well

03:49.506 --> 03:53.006 align:middle
above our 2 percent longer-run
goal and will likely continue

03:53.006 --> 03:54.896 align:middle
to do so well into next year.

03:55.426 --> 03:58.336 align:middle
While the drivers of
higher inflation have been

03:58.336 --> 04:00.926 align:middle
predominantly connected
to the dislocations caused

04:00.926 --> 04:03.936 align:middle
by the pandemic, price
increases have now spread

04:03.936 --> 04:06.276 align:middle
to a broader range of
goods and services.

04:07.056 --> 04:10.076 align:middle
Wages have also risen
briskly, but, thus far,

04:10.076 --> 04:12.666 align:middle
wage growth has not
been a major contributor

04:12.666 --> 04:14.596 align:middle
to the elevated levels
of inflation.

04:15.136 --> 04:19.046 align:middle
We are attentive to the risks
that persistent real wage growth

04:19.046 --> 04:21.646 align:middle
in excess of productivity
[growth] could put upward

04:21.936 --> 04:23.346 align:middle
pressure on inflation.

04:24.056 --> 04:26.756 align:middle
Like most forecasters, we
continue to expect inflation

04:26.756 --> 04:29.966 align:middle
to decline to levels closer to
our 2 percent longer-run goal

04:29.966 --> 04:31.096 align:middle
by the end of next year.

04:32.486 --> 04:35.716 align:middle
The median inflation projection
of FOMC participants falls

04:35.846 --> 04:39.986 align:middle
from 5.3 percent this year
to 2.6 percent next year;

04:40.956 --> 04:42.626 align:middle
this trajectory is
notably higher

04:42.626 --> 04:44.056 align:middle
than projected in September.

04:44.576 --> 04:48.746 align:middle
We understand that high
inflation imposes significant

04:48.746 --> 04:51.176 align:middle
hardship, especially
on those least able

04:51.176 --> 04:53.836 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

04:54.076 --> 04:55.576 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

04:56.866 --> 04:58.846 align:middle
We are committed to our
price-stability goal.

04:59.486 --> 05:01.936 align:middle
We will use our tools both
to support the economy

05:01.936 --> 05:05.106 align:middle
and a strong labor market and
to prevent higher inflation

05:05.106 --> 05:06.306 align:middle
from becoming entrenched.

05:06.796 --> 05:08.966 align:middle
We will be watching carefully

05:08.966 --> 05:11.186 align:middle
to see whether the
economy is evolving

05:11.186 --> 05:12.756 align:middle
in line with expectations.

05:14.676 --> 05:17.496 align:middle
The Fed's monetary policy
actions have been guided

05:17.496 --> 05:20.106 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

05:20.106 --> 05:22.046 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

05:22.636 --> 05:26.266 align:middle
In support of these goals,
the Committee reaffirmed the 0

05:26.266 --> 05:29.116 align:middle
to ¼ percent target range
for the federal funds rate.

05:30.256 --> 05:31.946 align:middle
We also updated our assessment

05:31.946 --> 05:34.876 align:middle
of the progress the economy
has made toward the criteria

05:34.876 --> 05:36.556 align:middle
specified in our
forward guidance

05:36.556 --> 05:37.596 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

05:38.956 --> 05:41.416 align:middle
With inflation having exceeded
2 percent for some time,

05:42.016 --> 05:43.906 align:middle
the Committee expects
it will be appropriate

05:43.906 --> 05:45.536 align:middle
to maintain this target range

05:46.056 --> 05:48.596 align:middle
until labor market conditions
have reached levels consistent

05:48.596 --> 05:50.816 align:middle
with the Committee's assessments
of maximum employment.

05:52.376 --> 05:54.206 align:middle
All FOMC participants forecast

05:54.206 --> 05:56.406 align:middle
that this remaining test
will be met next year.

05:56.826 --> 05:59.926 align:middle
The median projection
for the appropriate level

05:59.926 --> 06:04.126 align:middle
of the federal funds rate is
0.9 percent at the end of 2022,

06:04.776 --> 06:07.686 align:middle
about ½ percentage point higher
than projected in September.

06:08.946 --> 06:11.866 align:middle
Participants expect a gradual
pace of policy firming,

06:12.266 --> 06:14.666 align:middle
with the level of the federal
funds rate generally near

06:14.666 --> 06:17.936 align:middle
estimates of its longer-run
level by the end of 2024.

06:19.216 --> 06:21.596 align:middle
Of course, these projections
do not represent a Committee

06:21.596 --> 06:25.266 align:middle
decision or plan, and no
one knows with any certainty

06:25.266 --> 06:27.996 align:middle
where the economy will be
a year or more from now.

06:28.576 --> 06:32.876 align:middle
At today's meeting, the
Committee also decided

06:32.876 --> 06:35.576 align:middle
to double the pace of reductions
in its asset purchases.

06:36.146 --> 06:38.966 align:middle
Beginning in mid-January, we
will reduce the monthly pace

06:39.026 --> 06:42.196 align:middle
of our net asset
purchases by $20 billion

06:42.196 --> 06:44.646 align:middle
for Treasury securities
and $10 billion

06:44.646 --> 06:46.326 align:middle
for agency mortgage-backed
securities.

06:47.556 --> 06:50.816 align:middle
If the economy evolves broadly
as expected, similar reductions

06:50.816 --> 06:53.066 align:middle
in the pace of net asset
purchases will likely be

06:53.066 --> 06:56.056 align:middle
appropriate each month,
implying that increases

06:56.056 --> 06:57.736 align:middle
in our securities
holdings would cease

06:57.906 --> 07:00.136 align:middle
by mid-March-a few months sooner

07:00.136 --> 07:01.816 align:middle
than we anticipated
in early November.

07:02.026 --> 07:05.646 align:middle
We are phasing out our
purchases more rapidly because,

07:05.646 --> 07:07.476 align:middle
with elevated inflation
pressures

07:07.476 --> 07:09.376 align:middle
and a rapidly strengthening
labor market,

07:09.946 --> 07:12.316 align:middle
the economy no longer
needs increasing amounts

07:12.316 --> 07:13.296 align:middle
of policy support.

07:13.756 --> 07:16.456 align:middle
In addition, a quicker
conclusion

07:16.456 --> 07:19.006 align:middle
of our asset purchases
will better position policy

07:19.006 --> 07:22.186 align:middle
to address the full range of
plausible economic outcomes.

07:23.426 --> 07:26.506 align:middle
We remain prepared to adjust the
pace of purchases if warranted

07:26.576 --> 07:28.536 align:middle
by changes in the
economic outlook.

07:29.456 --> 07:31.926 align:middle
And, even after our balance
sheet stops expanding,

07:32.046 --> 07:33.806 align:middle
our holdings of securities
will continue

07:33.806 --> 07:36.606 align:middle
to foster accommodative
financial conditions.

07:38.066 --> 07:40.846 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

07:40.946 --> 07:43.256 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

07:44.206 --> 07:46.796 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:47.426 --> 07:51.416 align:middle
We at the Fed will do everything
we can to complete the recovery

07:51.416 --> 07:53.556 align:middle
in employment and achieve
our price-stability goal.

07:54.406 --> 07:54.766 align:middle
Thank you.

07:54.766 --> 07:55.946 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

07:57.046 --> 07:58.856 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:58.856 --> 08:01.386 align:middle
Mr. Chair, we'll go to Rachel
from the Washington Post.

08:01.556 --> 08:02.046 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

08:02.636 --> 08:03.686 align:middle
Thank you very much, Michelle.

08:03.686 --> 08:06.366 align:middle
And thank you, Chair Powell,
for taking our questions.

08:06.936 --> 08:09.586 align:middle
The latest FOMC materials say

08:09.586 --> 08:11.506 align:middle
that the FOMC thinks
it will be appropriate

08:11.506 --> 08:12.926 align:middle
to keep rates near zero

08:13.086 --> 08:15.806 align:middle
until labor market conditions
reach levels consistent

08:15.806 --> 08:16.746 align:middle
with maximum employment.

08:16.846 --> 08:19.486 align:middle
And there are also three
rate hikes penciled

08:19.486 --> 08:21.046 align:middle
in the projections
for next year.

08:21.576 --> 08:22.966 align:middle
In order to set up those hikes,

08:23.116 --> 08:25.416 align:middle
what will maximum
employment have to look like?

08:25.766 --> 08:28.346 align:middle
When will you know that
that threshold has been met?

08:28.576 --> 08:29.926 align:middle
And how will that
be communicated?

08:30.096 --> 08:30.556 align:middle
Thank you.

08:31.636 --> 08:31.976 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:31.976 --> 08:34.286 align:middle
So maximum employment, if
you look at our Statement

08:34.286 --> 08:36.776 align:middle
on Longer-Run Goals and
Monetary Policy Strategy,

08:37.346 --> 08:40.106 align:middle
maximum employment is
something that we look

08:40.106 --> 08:41.766 align:middle
at a broad range of indicators.

08:41.886 --> 08:43.736 align:middle
And those would include,
of course,

08:44.266 --> 08:45.776 align:middle
things like the unemployment
rate,

08:45.996 --> 08:50.146 align:middle
the labor force participation
rate, job openings, wages,

08:50.686 --> 08:52.736 align:middle
flows in and out
of the labor force

08:52.736 --> 08:54.656 align:middle
in various parts
of the labor force.

08:54.996 --> 08:58.466 align:middle
We'd also tend to look
broadly and inclusively

08:58.466 --> 09:00.916 align:middle
at different demographic groups
and not just at the headline

09:00.916 --> 09:01.726 align:middle
and aggregate numbers.

09:02.046 --> 09:05.416 align:middle
So that's a-that's a judgment
for the Committee to make.

09:05.636 --> 09:06.846 align:middle
The Committee will
make a judgment

09:06.846 --> 09:11.286 align:middle
that we've achieved labor
market conditions consistent

09:11.286 --> 09:15.076 align:middle
with maximum employment when it
makes that-it is, admittedly,

09:15.076 --> 09:17.286 align:middle
a judgment call because
it's a range of factors.

09:17.286 --> 09:20.616 align:middle
Unlike inflation, where we
have one number that sort

09:20.616 --> 09:23.196 align:middle
of dominates, it's a
broad range of things.

09:23.196 --> 09:28.976 align:middle
So, as I mentioned in my
opening remarks, in my view,

09:28.976 --> 09:32.306 align:middle
we are making rapid progress
toward maximum employment.

09:32.906 --> 09:34.996 align:middle
And you see that, of course,

09:34.996 --> 09:36.626 align:middle
in some of the factors
that I mentioned.

09:37.046 --> 09:39.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:40.166 --> 09:41.136 align:middle
Great, thank you.

09:41.866 --> 09:43.026 align:middle
Steve at CNBC.

09:43.026 --> 09:43.856 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

09:44.456 --> 09:44.806 align:middle
All right.

09:44.806 --> 09:46.196 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

09:46.556 --> 09:50.066 align:middle
My question is: If-It's
often said

09:50.066 --> 09:52.996 align:middle
that monetary policy has
long and variable lags.

09:53.056 --> 09:57.126 align:middle
How does continuing to buy
assets now, even though it's

09:57.126 --> 10:01.176 align:middle
at a slower pace, address the
current inflation problem?

10:01.536 --> 10:05.476 align:middle
Won't the impact of today's
changes not really have any

10:05.476 --> 10:07.556 align:middle
impact for six months
or a year down the road

10:07.556 --> 10:08.576 align:middle
on the current inflation
problem?

10:08.576 --> 10:11.126 align:middle
And aren't you actually
lengthening that time

10:11.126 --> 10:13.756 align:middle
by continuing to buy assets
such that it could be not

10:13.756 --> 10:17.306 align:middle
until the long and variable lag
after you end purchases sometime

10:17.306 --> 10:19.556 align:middle
in March that you'll
start to have any impact

10:19.556 --> 10:20.526 align:middle
on the inflation problem?

10:21.616 --> 10:22.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:22.216 --> 10:25.316 align:middle
So, on the first part of
your question, which is,

10:25.316 --> 10:27.896 align:middle
"Why not stop purchasing
now?," I would just say this:

10:27.896 --> 10:31.536 align:middle
We've learned that, in dealing
with balance sheet issues,

10:31.536 --> 10:34.346 align:middle
we've learned that it's
best to take a careful sort

10:34.346 --> 10:36.956 align:middle
of methodical approach
to make adjustments.

10:37.436 --> 10:39.346 align:middle
Markets can be sensitive to it.

10:39.706 --> 10:41.726 align:middle
And we thought that this
was-this was a doubling

10:41.726 --> 10:42.396 align:middle
of the speed.

10:42.396 --> 10:44.756 align:middle
We're basically two
meetings away now

10:44.756 --> 10:46.926 align:middle
from finishing the taper.

10:46.926 --> 10:48.666 align:middle
And we thought that was
the appropriate way to go.

10:49.266 --> 10:52.566 align:middle
So we announced it, and
that's what will happen.

10:53.126 --> 10:55.356 align:middle
You know, the question of
long and variable lags is,

10:55.356 --> 10:56.156 align:middle
is an interesting one.

10:56.156 --> 10:58.216 align:middle
That's, that's Milton
Friedman's famous statement.

10:58.216 --> 11:02.196 align:middle
And I do think that
in this world,

11:02.196 --> 11:05.036 align:middle
where everything is-where the
global financial markets are

11:05.036 --> 11:08.066 align:middle
connected together, financial
conditions can change

11:08.096 --> 11:08.826 align:middle
very quickly.

11:09.226 --> 11:12.266 align:middle
And my own sense is that they
get into-financial conditions

11:12.266 --> 11:14.266 align:middle
affect the economy
fairly rapidly,

11:14.976 --> 11:16.796 align:middle
longer than the traditional
thought of,

11:16.796 --> 11:18.626 align:middle
you know, a year or 18 months.

11:18.626 --> 11:19.586 align:middle
Shorter than that, rather.

11:20.186 --> 11:23.846 align:middle
But in addition,
when we communicate

11:23.916 --> 11:25.666 align:middle
about what we're going to do,

11:26.156 --> 11:27.796 align:middle
the markets move immediately
[in response] to that.

11:27.796 --> 11:32.206 align:middle
So financial conditions are
changing to reflect, you know,

11:32.206 --> 11:36.516 align:middle
the forecasts that we made and
basically, which was, I think,

11:36.516 --> 11:38.596 align:middle
fairly in line with what
markets were expecting.

11:39.106 --> 11:42.256 align:middle
But financial conditions
don't wait to change until,

11:42.256 --> 11:43.516 align:middle
until things actually happen.

11:43.996 --> 11:47.116 align:middle
They, they change on the
expectation of things happening.

11:47.116 --> 11:50.366 align:middle
So I don't think it's a
question of having to wait.

11:51.196 --> 11:51.936 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

11:51.936 --> 11:53.476 align:middle
Can I just follow up?

11:53.476 --> 11:57.636 align:middle
Thinking about having to
wait, is it still the policy,

11:57.636 --> 11:58.906 align:middle
or the position,
of the Committee

11:58.906 --> 12:03.166 align:middle
that you will not raise rates
until the taper is complete?

12:03.166 --> 12:03.606 align:middle
Thank you.

12:05.536 --> 12:06.086 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:06.286 --> 12:08.936 align:middle
Yes. The sense of
that, of course,

12:08.966 --> 12:12.316 align:middle
being that buying assets
is adding accommodation

12:12.316 --> 12:14.586 align:middle
and raising rates is
removing accommodation.

12:15.286 --> 12:18.256 align:middle
Since we're two meetings away
from completing the taper,

12:18.746 --> 12:23.766 align:middle
assuming things go as, as
expected, I think if we wanted

12:23.766 --> 12:25.766 align:middle
to lift off before then, then,

12:25.766 --> 12:29.086 align:middle
then what we-you would stop
the taper potentially sooner.

12:29.086 --> 12:31.026 align:middle
But it's not something
I expect to happen.

12:31.536 --> 12:34.676 align:middle
But I do-I do not think
it would be appropriate,

12:34.676 --> 12:36.756 align:middle
and we-and we don't find
ourselves in a situation

12:36.756 --> 12:38.176 align:middle
where we-where we might have

12:38.636 --> 12:42.296 align:middle
to raise rates while we're
still purchasing assets.

12:42.786 --> 12:45.676 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:45.676 --> 12:47.106 align:middle
Okay, let's go to
Colby at the FT.

12:47.106 --> 12:48.396 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

12:49.606 --> 12:50.636 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

12:50.636 --> 12:53.236 align:middle
Chair Powell, I'm curious
exactly how much distance you

12:53.236 --> 12:55.796 align:middle
think there should be
between the end of the taper

12:55.796 --> 12:57.596 align:middle
and the first interest
rate increase.

12:57.596 --> 13:00.686 align:middle
Back in 2014, the guidance
was-that was given was

13:00.686 --> 13:03.256 align:middle
for the fed funds rate to
remain at the target level

13:03.386 --> 13:05.776 align:middle
for a considerable
time after the end

13:05.776 --> 13:07.286 align:middle
of the asset purchase program.

13:07.286 --> 13:09.406 align:middle
Is that an approach
you support now?

13:09.406 --> 13:11.716 align:middle
Or does the current economic
situation warrant something a

13:11.716 --> 13:12.336 align:middle
bit different?

13:12.336 --> 13:12.886 align:middle
Thank you.

13:13.586 --> 13:13.796 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:13.796 --> 13:17.476 align:middle
So we, we haven't made any
decision of that nature.

13:17.576 --> 13:19.726 align:middle
And so, no, I wouldn't say
that's our position at all.

13:19.726 --> 13:21.246 align:middle
We really haven't taken
a position on that.

13:21.746 --> 13:26.636 align:middle
I will say that we did
talk today-We had our first

13:26.636 --> 13:28.886 align:middle
discussion about the
balance sheet, for example.

13:28.886 --> 13:33.666 align:middle
And we went through
the way-the sequence

13:33.726 --> 13:37.416 align:middle
of events regarding the
runoff and that sort of thing

13:37.416 --> 13:38.906 align:middle
with the balance
sheet, last time.

13:39.186 --> 13:42.676 align:middle
And I think people thought that
was an interesting discussion.

13:42.876 --> 13:45.646 align:middle
They thought that it was
informative, but people pointed

13:45.646 --> 13:50.056 align:middle
out that this is a significantly
different economic situation

13:50.056 --> 13:51.456 align:middle
that we have at the
current time,

13:51.456 --> 13:55.396 align:middle
and that those-the differences
that we see now would tend

13:55.396 --> 13:58.186 align:middle
to influence how we think
about the balance sheet.

13:58.186 --> 14:00.336 align:middle
And the same thing would be
true about raising rates.

14:00.906 --> 14:04.756 align:middle
I don't-I don't foresee that
there would be that kind

14:04.756 --> 14:06.866 align:middle
of very extended
wait at this time.

14:06.866 --> 14:08.536 align:middle
The economy is so much stronger.

14:08.536 --> 14:11.406 align:middle
I was here at the Fed when
we lifted off the last time.

14:11.406 --> 14:13.046 align:middle
And the economy is
so much stronger now,

14:13.046 --> 14:15.436 align:middle
so much closer to
full employment.

14:15.436 --> 14:17.166 align:middle
Inflation is running
well above target,

14:17.166 --> 14:18.706 align:middle
and growth is well
above potential.

14:19.506 --> 14:22.256 align:middle
There wouldn't be the need
for that kind of long delay.

14:22.256 --> 14:26.576 align:middle
Having said that, I-you know,
we'll make this decision

14:27.376 --> 14:29.986 align:middle
in coming meetings, and
it's not-it's not a decision

14:29.986 --> 14:31.596 align:middle
that the Committee has
really focused on yet.

14:32.046 --> 14:35.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:35.386 --> 14:37.006 align:middle
Let's go to Nick at the
Wall Street Journal.

14:37.566 --> 14:41.316 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

14:41.316 --> 14:44.956 align:middle
Thank you, Nick Timiraos
at the Wall Street Journal.

14:44.956 --> 14:47.406 align:middle
Chair Powell, in March
you answered a question

14:47.406 --> 14:49.176 align:middle
about maximum employment
like this:

14:49.176 --> 14:52.456 align:middle
You said 4 percent would
be a nice unemployment rate

14:52.456 --> 14:55.286 align:middle
to get to, but it'll take
more than that to get

14:55.286 --> 14:56.506 align:middle
to maximum employment.

14:56.976 --> 15:00.486 align:middle
More recently, you have hinted
at a possible distinction

15:00.486 --> 15:04.476 align:middle
between the level of maximum
employment that's achievable

15:04.476 --> 15:06.406 align:middle
in the short run
versus in the long run.

15:07.066 --> 15:08.986 align:middle
Has your view of the level

15:08.986 --> 15:11.666 align:middle
of maximum employment
changed this year?

15:11.666 --> 15:12.656 align:middle
And if so, how?

15:13.196 --> 15:16.246 align:middle
And how close is the economy
right now to your judgment

15:16.246 --> 15:19.196 align:middle
of the short-run level
of maximum employment?

15:19.366 --> 15:19.836 align:middle
Thank you.

15:19.836 --> 15:20.346 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:20.346 --> 15:24.136 align:middle
Right. So the-you know, the
thing is, we're not going back

15:24.136 --> 15:26.816 align:middle
to the same economy we
had in February of 2020.

15:26.816 --> 15:29.866 align:middle
And I think early on,
that was-the sense was

15:29.926 --> 15:31.376 align:middle
that that's where
we were headed.

15:31.766 --> 15:34.446 align:middle
The post-pandemic labor
market, and the economy

15:34.446 --> 15:35.506 align:middle
in general, will be different.

15:36.096 --> 15:39.186 align:middle
And the maximum level of
employment that's consistent

15:39.306 --> 15:41.616 align:middle
with price stability
evolves over time

15:41.616 --> 15:44.896 align:middle
within a-within a business
cycle and over a longer period,

15:45.356 --> 15:48.396 align:middle
in part reflecting [the]
evolution of the factors

15:48.396 --> 15:50.016 align:middle
that affect labor supply,

15:50.136 --> 15:51.656 align:middle
including those related
to the pandemic.

15:52.386 --> 15:55.786 align:middle
So I would say: Look, we're,
we're at 4.2 percent now

15:55.786 --> 15:58.226 align:middle
and it's been-the unemployment
rate has been dropping

15:58.226 --> 15:58.976 align:middle
very quickly.

15:59.516 --> 16:01.626 align:middle
So we're already in the
vicinity of 4 percent.

16:02.186 --> 16:08.806 align:middle
The way in which-The
important metric

16:08.806 --> 16:12.646 align:middle
that has been disappointing
really has been labor force

16:12.646 --> 16:13.786 align:middle
participation, of course,

16:13.786 --> 16:18.806 align:middle
where we had widely thought-I
had certainly thought-that,

16:18.806 --> 16:23.366 align:middle
last fall, as unemployment
insurance ran off,

16:23.426 --> 16:26.306 align:middle
as vaccinations increased,
[and] as schools reopened,

16:26.726 --> 16:31.046 align:middle
that we would see a
significant surge, if you will,

16:31.046 --> 16:34.366 align:middle
or at least a surge, in
labor force participation.

16:34.526 --> 16:36.296 align:middle
So we've begun to
see some improvement.

16:36.296 --> 16:38.946 align:middle
We certainly welcome the
2/10 improvement that we got

16:39.036 --> 16:40.586 align:middle
in the November report.

16:40.586 --> 16:47.156 align:middle
But I do think that it's-it
feels likely now that the return

16:47.156 --> 16:50.546 align:middle
to higher participation
is going to take longer.

16:50.826 --> 16:52.646 align:middle
And, in fact, that's,
that's been the pattern

16:52.646 --> 16:55.836 align:middle
in past cycles-that labor
force participation has tended

16:55.836 --> 17:00.106 align:middle
to recover in the wake of a
strong recovery in unemployment,

17:00.106 --> 17:01.206 align:middle
which is what we're
getting right now.

17:01.546 --> 17:05.256 align:middle
So you-it could well have been
that this cycle was different

17:05.256 --> 17:07.086 align:middle
because of the short
nature of it

17:07.546 --> 17:10.456 align:middle
and the very strong-the number
of job openings, for example,

17:10.456 --> 17:12.146 align:middle
you would have thought that
that would have pulled people

17:12.146 --> 17:12.506 align:middle
back in.

17:12.506 --> 17:15.386 align:middle
But, really, it's the pandemic,
it's a range of factors.

17:15.416 --> 17:18.096 align:middle
But the reality is, we don't
have a strong labor force

17:18.096 --> 17:20.536 align:middle
participation recovery yet,

17:20.756 --> 17:22.116 align:middle
and we may not have
it for some time.

17:22.176 --> 17:24.146 align:middle
At the same time, we
have to make policy now.

17:24.726 --> 17:27.556 align:middle
And inflation is
well above target.

17:28.036 --> 17:31.026 align:middle
So this is something we need
to take into-take into account.

17:31.526 --> 17:33.196 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

17:33.286 --> 17:36.226 align:middle
If I could-if I could follow
up, you've talked recently

17:36.226 --> 17:37.456 align:middle
about risk management.

17:37.636 --> 17:41.256 align:middle
And so does that mean that the
Committee might feel compelled

17:41.256 --> 17:44.426 align:middle
to raise interest rates
before you're convinced

17:44.726 --> 17:47.456 align:middle
that you've achieved
the employment test

17:47.456 --> 17:48.706 align:middle
in your forward guidance?

17:48.706 --> 17:49.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:49.696 --> 17:51.456 align:middle
So this is-this is
not at all a decision

17:51.456 --> 17:52.546 align:middle
that the Committee has made.

17:52.776 --> 17:54.206 align:middle
But you're really
asking a question

17:54.206 --> 17:55.956 align:middle
about how our framework works.

17:56.416 --> 17:58.576 align:middle
And, yes, there is
a-there's a provision,

17:58.896 --> 18:01.636 align:middle
it used to be called the
"balanced approach" provision,

18:02.186 --> 18:09.066 align:middle
that says, in effect, that in
situations in which the pursuit

18:09.066 --> 18:10.966 align:middle
of the maximum-employment goal

18:10.966 --> 18:12.376 align:middle
and [the pursuit of] the
price-stability goal are not

18:12.376 --> 18:16.626 align:middle
complementary, we have to
take account of the distance

18:16.736 --> 18:18.906 align:middle
from the goal and
the-and the speed

18:18.906 --> 18:19.936 align:middle
at which we're approaching it.

18:20.366 --> 18:23.496 align:middle
And so that is, in effect,
an "off-ramp" which could,

18:23.496 --> 18:27.686 align:middle
in concept, be taken and
it's in our framework,

18:27.686 --> 18:29.006 align:middle
it's been in our
framework a long time.

18:29.006 --> 18:30.566 align:middle
I've talked about it on
a number of occasions.

18:30.566 --> 18:35.536 align:middle
It, it is a provision that would
enable us to, in this case,

18:35.536 --> 18:36.876 align:middle
because of high inflation,

18:37.226 --> 18:39.976 align:middle
move before achieving
maximum employment.

18:40.126 --> 18:41.986 align:middle
Now, we're-as I said,

18:41.986 --> 18:45.616 align:middle
we're making rapid progress
toward maximum employment

18:45.616 --> 18:47.906 align:middle
in my-in my thinking,
in my opinion.

18:48.666 --> 18:51.676 align:middle
And I don't at all know
that we will-that we'll have

18:51.676 --> 18:53.686 align:middle
to invoke that, that paragraph.

18:53.686 --> 18:55.626 align:middle
But just as a factual
matter, that is part

18:55.626 --> 18:58.126 align:middle
of our framework-and has been,
really, for a very long time.

18:58.346 --> 18:59.266 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

18:59.506 --> 18:59.896 align:middle
Thank you.

18:59.896 --> 19:00.636 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:01.266 --> 19:03.306 align:middle
Thanks. We'll go to
Howard at Reuters.

19:05.446 --> 19:06.326 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

19:06.406 --> 19:07.676 align:middle
Thanks, as usual.

19:07.806 --> 19:09.496 align:middle
So I guess I gotta ask about
the elephant in the room,

19:09.496 --> 19:12.196 align:middle
which is the Omicron variant.

19:12.256 --> 19:13.956 align:middle
You know, this seems to
already be pushing one

19:13.956 --> 19:17.526 align:middle
of your colleagues, the Bank
of England, off its course.

19:17.916 --> 19:19.786 align:middle
Things have evolved
very fast there.

19:19.786 --> 19:23.206 align:middle
Hasn't quite hit the shores
of the U.S. in full force yet.

19:23.206 --> 19:24.866 align:middle
But people seem to expect it to.

19:24.866 --> 19:29.216 align:middle
So I'm wondering, in
your feelings about this,

19:29.216 --> 19:30.806 align:middle
are you convinced
that this is going

19:30.806 --> 19:32.846 align:middle
to be perhaps a more infectious,

19:32.846 --> 19:35.236 align:middle
but less serious,
variant of the virus?

19:35.236 --> 19:36.536 align:middle
Or are you simply confident

19:36.536 --> 19:39.956 align:middle
that the U.S. economy
can continue its divorce

19:39.956 --> 19:40.776 align:middle
from the pandemic?

19:41.256 --> 19:43.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:43.576 --> 19:45.456 align:middle
Well, I think there's
a lot of uncertainty,

19:45.456 --> 19:48.276 align:middle
which is why we-why we called
it out in our statement,

19:48.276 --> 19:52.566 align:middle
our postmeeting statement,
as a risk.

19:52.996 --> 19:56.816 align:middle
It's-we, we follow the
same experts and I-we,

19:56.816 --> 19:59.596 align:middle
we talk privately to the same
experts that everyone else does

19:59.596 --> 20:01.656 align:middle
and read the same
articles in the paper

20:01.656 --> 20:03.546 align:middle
and the same research
and, you know,

20:03.546 --> 20:07.016 align:middle
so the-you mentioned the
early assessment is highly

20:07.016 --> 20:08.806 align:middle
transmissible, perhaps
not as severe,

20:09.046 --> 20:12.636 align:middle
some continuing protection
from, from existing vaccines

20:12.636 --> 20:15.016 align:middle
and also existing immunity
from having had the disease.

20:15.796 --> 20:17.266 align:middle
That's a first draft.

20:17.266 --> 20:20.426 align:middle
We're a long way from knowing
what it will turn out to be.

20:20.936 --> 20:24.496 align:middle
It may well come to the United
States and replace Delta

20:24.496 --> 20:26.636 align:middle
as the dominant variant
fairly quickly.

20:26.636 --> 20:28.206 align:middle
That could easily happen.

20:28.746 --> 20:31.116 align:middle
I think there's another
step there,

20:31.116 --> 20:34.256 align:middle
though-which is what's going to
be the effect on the economy.

20:34.256 --> 20:38.496 align:middle
And that will depend, you know,
on how much it suppresses demand

20:38.496 --> 20:40.536 align:middle
as opposed to suppressing
supply.

20:40.896 --> 20:43.746 align:middle
It is not clear how big
the effects would be

20:43.746 --> 20:49.206 align:middle
on either inflation or
growth or hiring on top

20:49.206 --> 20:52.886 align:middle
of what's already going on,
which is quite a strong,

20:52.886 --> 20:55.646 align:middle
you know, wave of Delta
that's hitting large parts

20:55.646 --> 20:57.566 align:middle
of the country across the
northern United States

20:57.566 --> 21:00.476 align:middle
and all the way to the eastern
seaboard and now coming down.

21:00.476 --> 21:02.656 align:middle
We're having quite
a wave of Delta.

21:02.656 --> 21:05.996 align:middle
So coming in on top of that,
again, it's very difficult

21:05.996 --> 21:07.706 align:middle
to say what the economic
effects would be.

21:07.706 --> 21:09.516 align:middle
I do think, wave upon wave,

21:09.966 --> 21:11.536 align:middle
people are learning
to live with this.

21:12.006 --> 21:13.766 align:middle
More and more people
are getting vaccinated.

21:13.826 --> 21:16.976 align:middle
So [vaccinated] people
who get the new variant,

21:17.866 --> 21:20.736 align:middle
it affects them much less
than it tends to affect,

21:20.736 --> 21:22.586 align:middle
in the aggregate, people
who are not vaccinated.

21:22.586 --> 21:23.826 align:middle
So the more people
get vaccinated,

21:23.826 --> 21:25.076 align:middle
the less the economic effect.

21:25.376 --> 21:27.586 align:middle
It doesn't mean it won't
have an economic effect.

21:27.616 --> 21:30.916 align:middle
Delta had an effect of
slowing down hiring.

21:30.916 --> 21:34.986 align:middle
And it actually-it had an
effect on global supply chains.

21:35.506 --> 21:38.616 align:middle
And that sort of-that
hurt the process

21:38.616 --> 21:41.186 align:middle
of the global supply
chains getting worked out.

21:41.776 --> 21:43.486 align:middle
So it can have an
economic effect.

21:43.486 --> 21:45.446 align:middle
I just think, at this
point, we don't know much.

21:45.956 --> 21:48.176 align:middle
We'll know a whole lot more in
three weeks, and we'll know more

21:48.176 --> 21:48.986 align:middle
than that in six weeks.

21:49.806 --> 21:50.406 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

21:50.406 --> 21:52.446 align:middle
But if I can follow
up: You're clearly,

21:52.446 --> 21:55.436 align:middle
sort of from a risk standpoint,
comfortable putting away one

21:55.436 --> 21:56.956 align:middle
of your tools pretty soon.

21:56.956 --> 22:00.486 align:middle
Which sort of implies that
whatever Omicron brings,

22:00.486 --> 22:03.096 align:middle
you're comfortable the
economy can handle it

22:03.096 --> 22:04.846 align:middle
without quantitative easing.

22:04.846 --> 22:05.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:05.986 --> 22:09.746 align:middle
Yes. Yes, look, I think-if you
look at the state of the economy

22:10.256 --> 22:13.676 align:middle
and the amount-the strength
of demand, the strength

22:13.676 --> 22:16.226 align:middle
of just overall demand, and the
strength of demand for labor,

22:16.226 --> 22:19.876 align:middle
look at inflation,
look at wages.

22:19.876 --> 22:24.166 align:middle
I think moving, you know, moving
forward the end of our taper

22:24.366 --> 22:28.796 align:middle
by a few months is really-is
really an appropriate thing

22:28.796 --> 22:29.156 align:middle
to do.

22:29.156 --> 22:33.596 align:middle
And I think, really, Omicron
doesn't really have much

22:33.896 --> 22:34.446 align:middle
to do with that.

22:34.446 --> 22:35.206 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

22:35.206 --> 22:36.126 align:middle
Thank you.

22:36.546 --> 22:37.646 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:37.816 --> 22:39.456 align:middle
Let's go to Jeanna at
the New York Times.

22:39.566 --> 22:40.006 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

22:40.166 --> 22:43.196 align:middle
Hey, Chairman Powell.

22:43.196 --> 22:44.926 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

22:44.926 --> 22:47.406 align:middle
I wonder if you could
talk a little bit

22:47.406 --> 22:50.576 align:middle
about what prompted your recent
pivot toward greater wariness

22:50.576 --> 22:51.516 align:middle
around inflation.

22:52.766 --> 22:53.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:53.586 --> 22:57.686 align:middle
Sure. So I guess I would
go back and-you know,

22:57.686 --> 22:59.716 align:middle
it's been a continual
process, really.

23:00.486 --> 23:03.586 align:middle
Inflation really
popped up, right?

23:03.586 --> 23:06.036 align:middle
In the late spring last year.

23:06.576 --> 23:09.076 align:middle
And we had a view-it was
very, very widely held

23:09.076 --> 23:10.696 align:middle
in the forecasting
community-that this would

23:10.696 --> 23:11.296 align:middle
be temporary.

23:11.296 --> 23:14.766 align:middle
It was quite narrow, you
know, a limited number

23:14.886 --> 23:17.846 align:middle
of factors were causing it.

23:18.216 --> 23:21.786 align:middle
And there was a decent amount of
evidence to support that view,

23:22.016 --> 23:26.486 align:middle
that it would be temporary
or transitory, as we said.

23:26.736 --> 23:29.766 align:middle
Certainly, we had five
months of declining month

23:29.766 --> 23:32.796 align:middle
on month-monthly
readings of inflation,

23:32.796 --> 23:36.356 align:middle
but we didn't see much in the
way of progress on labor supply

23:36.356 --> 23:38.426 align:middle
or on other supply-side issues.

23:38.896 --> 23:41.306 align:middle
Then in September, I'd
say after Labor Day,

23:41.306 --> 23:43.536 align:middle
we started to see-it
started to become clear

23:43.536 --> 23:45.786 align:middle
that this was both
larger in its effect

23:45.786 --> 23:48.666 align:middle
on inflation and
more persistent.

23:48.666 --> 23:51.386 align:middle
And, of course, I said
so on many occasions,

23:51.386 --> 23:53.336 align:middle
and one of the consequences
of that is

23:53.336 --> 23:54.876 align:middle
that we move the taper forward.

23:54.976 --> 23:57.406 align:middle
We moved the taper forward and
we-and it's a much faster taper

23:57.406 --> 23:58.446 align:middle
than had been planned.

23:58.446 --> 24:01.156 align:middle
So we've been adapting-I've
been saying we're adapting

24:01.156 --> 24:01.766 align:middle
our policy.

24:01.936 --> 24:05.396 align:middle
So coming to your real
question, we got the ECI reading

24:05.396 --> 24:08.796 align:middle
on the eve of the November
meeting-it was the Friday before

24:08.796 --> 24:13.106 align:middle
the November meeting-and it was
very high, 5.7 percent reading

24:13.216 --> 24:16.216 align:middle
for the employment compensation
index for the third quarter,

24:16.216 --> 24:17.756 align:middle
not annualized, for
the third quarter,

24:18.186 --> 24:19.176 align:middle
just before the meeting.

24:19.736 --> 24:23.996 align:middle
And I thought for a second there
whether we-whether we should

24:24.136 --> 24:25.726 align:middle
increase our taper,
[We] decided to go ahead

24:25.726 --> 24:28.196 align:middle
with what we had-what
we had "socialized."

24:28.776 --> 24:31.186 align:middle
Then, right after that,
we got the next Friday

24:31.186 --> 24:32.746 align:middle
after the meeting, two
days after the meeting,

24:32.746 --> 24:36.516 align:middle
we got a very strong
employment report and, you know,

24:36.516 --> 24:40.906 align:middle
revisions to prior readings and,
and no increase in labor supply.

24:41.106 --> 24:42.846 align:middle
And the Friday after
that, we got the CPI,

24:42.986 --> 24:44.866 align:middle
which was a very
hot, high reading.

24:45.396 --> 24:48.046 align:middle
And I, honestly, at that
point, really decided

24:48.236 --> 24:52.266 align:middle
that I thought we needed
to-we needed to look at,

24:52.266 --> 24:53.256 align:middle
at speeding up the taper.

24:53.476 --> 24:55.366 align:middle
And we went to work on that.

24:55.366 --> 24:56.996 align:middle
So that's, that's
really what happened.

24:56.996 --> 25:02.786 align:middle
It was essentially higher
inflation and faster-turns

25:02.786 --> 25:05.266 align:middle
out much faster progress
in the labor market.

25:05.506 --> 25:08.236 align:middle
Really, what's happening is the
unemployment rate is catching

25:08.236 --> 25:10.696 align:middle
up-seems to be catching up-with
a lot of the other readings

25:10.696 --> 25:13.866 align:middle
of a tight labor market, 6/10
[decline] over one cycle.

25:14.356 --> 25:18.626 align:middle
So that's really what happened
and, you know, widely supported

25:18.626 --> 25:19.806 align:middle
in the Committee today,

25:19.806 --> 25:21.556 align:middle
as you can see-unanimous
vote-but beyond

25:21.556 --> 25:23.916 align:middle
that I think widely,
widely supported,

25:23.916 --> 25:25.066 align:middle
you know, this, this move.

25:25.066 --> 25:25.556 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

25:26.096 --> 25:28.416 align:middle
If I could just follow
up quickly,

25:28.416 --> 25:30.646 align:middle
you noted that the ECI
was one of the things

25:30.646 --> 25:32.526 align:middle
that made you nervous,
but you also said earlier

25:32.526 --> 25:33.476 align:middle
that you don't see signs

25:33.476 --> 25:36.716 align:middle
that wages are actually
factoring into inflation yet.

25:36.716 --> 25:39.106 align:middle
And I guess I wonder
how you think about sort

25:39.106 --> 25:41.756 align:middle
of the wage picture as, as
you're making these assessments.

25:41.756 --> 25:42.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:42.986 --> 25:46.166 align:middle
Right. So-but I said-you,
you quoted me correctly.

25:46.166 --> 25:49.506 align:middle
It's, it's-so far, we don't
see-wages are not a big part

25:49.506 --> 25:51.606 align:middle
of the high-inflation
story that we're seeing.

25:52.166 --> 25:54.716 align:middle
As you look forward,
let's assume

25:54.716 --> 25:57.336 align:middle
that the goods economy
does sort itself out,

25:57.516 --> 25:59.896 align:middle
and supply chains
get working again,

25:59.896 --> 26:01.706 align:middle
and maybe there's a
rebalancing back to services

26:01.706 --> 26:04.426 align:middle
and all that kind of thing.

26:04.656 --> 26:10.226 align:middle
But what, what that leaves
behind is the other things

26:10.556 --> 26:13.156 align:middle
that can lead to
persistent inflation.

26:13.156 --> 26:16.576 align:middle
In particular-we don't see this
yet, but if you had something

26:16.576 --> 26:18.806 align:middle
where wages were
persistently-[increases in] real

26:18.806 --> 26:21.116 align:middle
wages were persistently
above productivity growth,

26:22.246 --> 26:27.226 align:middle
that puts upward pressure on,
on firms, and they raise prices.

26:27.726 --> 26:30.026 align:middle
It would take something
that was persistent

26:30.026 --> 26:32.316 align:middle
and material for that to happen.

26:32.316 --> 26:33.496 align:middle
And we don't see that yet.

26:33.566 --> 26:36.826 align:middle
But with the kind of hot labor
market readings-wages we're

26:36.826 --> 26:39.326 align:middle
seeing, it's something that
we're-that we're watching.

26:39.796 --> 26:41.606 align:middle
And the other thing, of
course, is, is rents,

26:41.996 --> 26:43.126 align:middle
which are very important.

26:43.126 --> 26:44.816 align:middle
You know, owners'
equivalent rent.

26:44.816 --> 26:47.186 align:middle
That's another thing which is
very economically sensitive.

26:47.186 --> 26:51.066 align:middle
Unlike the things that are
causing the inflation now,

26:51.066 --> 26:53.186 align:middle
this is economically
sensitive-and

26:53.186 --> 26:55.046 align:middle
so would be expected to move up.

26:55.046 --> 26:58.826 align:middle
And so as the-as some things
go down, the question is:

26:58.826 --> 27:01.156 align:middle
Where will we be when we come
out the other side of this?

27:01.156 --> 27:02.876 align:middle
And we need to keep our
eyes on those things.

27:02.876 --> 27:03.506 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

27:04.336 --> 27:04.646 align:middle
Thank you.

27:04.646 --> 27:05.306 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:06.516 --> 27:09.886 align:middle
Thanks. We'll go
to Chris at the AP.

27:11.996 --> 27:12.826 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

27:12.826 --> 27:13.496 align:middle
Thank you.

27:14.316 --> 27:19.776 align:middle
Well, next year, you
could see growth slowing,

27:19.776 --> 27:23.786 align:middle
and you could see some
disinflation, particularly

27:23.786 --> 27:26.086 align:middle
if the Omicron variant
does spread more widely.

27:26.706 --> 27:29.946 align:middle
Would you delay rate
hikes in that situation?

27:29.946 --> 27:32.516 align:middle
And how would you
think about that?

27:32.976 --> 27:35.376 align:middle
And also, how would
you explain rate hikes

27:35.376 --> 27:38.896 align:middle
if you do follow through,
in that kind of situation,

27:38.896 --> 27:41.136 align:middle
with inflation fading
and growth slowing?

27:41.136 --> 27:43.506 align:middle
How would you explain
rate hikes to the public,

27:43.836 --> 27:46.016 align:middle
particularly those who may
still be looking for work?

27:46.016 --> 27:46.646 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:47.026 --> 27:51.346 align:middle
Well, as you know, you
know, the SEP is not a plan.

27:51.346 --> 27:53.606 align:middle
It's not something we
debate, negotiate over,

27:53.606 --> 27:56.406 align:middle
or really discuss in terms of
what the right answer should be.

27:56.406 --> 28:01.216 align:middle
People write down their, their
assumptions-their assessment

28:01.216 --> 28:04.876 align:middle
of appropriate policy based
on their economic forecast.

28:04.876 --> 28:07.316 align:middle
And so the median-what
you're talking about,

28:07.316 --> 28:09.096 align:middle
the three rate increases,
that's the median

28:09.096 --> 28:10.546 align:middle
of the Committee-the median
of the Committee is also

28:10.546 --> 28:11.936 align:middle
that growth will be 4 percent

28:11.936 --> 28:14.236 align:middle
and then unemployment
will be 3½ percent

28:14.236 --> 28:15.046 align:middle
by the end of the year.

28:15.266 --> 28:16.996 align:middle
So that's a really
strong economy.

28:17.636 --> 28:20.086 align:middle
And, of course, if
the-if the economy turns

28:20.086 --> 28:23.376 align:middle
out to be quite different from
that, then so will the rate.

28:23.376 --> 28:27.556 align:middle
You know, we-no one will say,
"Oh, we can't change our policy

28:27.556 --> 28:30.136 align:middle
because we wrote
something down in December."

28:30.256 --> 28:31.866 align:middle
No one's ever said that or will.

28:32.236 --> 28:35.336 align:middle
It will-the actual rate
decisions we make will depend

28:35.336 --> 28:38.256 align:middle
on our evolving assessment
of the forecast.

28:38.606 --> 28:41.746 align:middle
So, for example, if, if
the economy were to slow

28:41.746 --> 28:43.436 align:middle
down significantly,
then you would expect

28:43.436 --> 28:46.346 align:middle
that that would have an effect
of slowing down rate increases.

28:46.666 --> 28:48.816 align:middle
But we'd have to-you know,
we look at our two goals,

28:49.126 --> 28:50.706 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability,

28:50.706 --> 28:54.446 align:middle
and we make policy based on
them, and not with respect

28:54.446 --> 28:56.616 align:middle
to what we wrote
down in a prior SEP.

28:56.666 --> 28:57.706 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:00.236 --> 29:00.936 align:middle
Thank you.

29:00.936 --> 29:03.326 align:middle
Let's go to Victoria
Guida at Politico.

29:03.946 --> 29:05.736 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

29:05.906 --> 29:06.986 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

29:06.986 --> 29:09.806 align:middle
I wanted to follow up
on maximum employment.

29:09.806 --> 29:13.606 align:middle
So, you know, the new framework
was basically designed,

29:13.876 --> 29:17.446 align:middle
as I understand it, so that
there was a de-emphasis

29:17.446 --> 29:19.876 align:middle
on guessing where
inflation would pick

29:19.876 --> 29:20.886 align:middle
up because of employment.

29:20.886 --> 29:23.646 align:middle
And, basically, you were going
to wait until you saw the whites

29:23.646 --> 29:24.636 align:middle
of the eyes of inflation.

29:24.636 --> 29:26.226 align:middle
And that's how you
would kind of know

29:26.226 --> 29:27.906 align:middle
that you reached
maximum employment.

29:28.086 --> 29:31.356 align:middle
So, I'm wondering, you
know, you've talked a lot

29:31.356 --> 29:32.256 align:middle
about the different ways

29:32.256 --> 29:34.106 align:middle
that you might measure
maximum employment.

29:34.196 --> 29:38.336 align:middle
But from what I understand,
that's still basically:

29:38.336 --> 29:41.846 align:middle
The way that you know that
you're there is inflation.

29:41.846 --> 29:44.636 align:middle
So is that understanding
correct?

29:45.056 --> 29:50.486 align:middle
And how is-are those
signals likely

29:50.486 --> 29:51.906 align:middle
to be clear right now given

29:51.906 --> 29:55.556 align:middle
that you have inflation
that's caused by, you know,

29:55.556 --> 29:58.136 align:middle
these supply chain disruptions
that might also lead to,

29:58.136 --> 30:01.016 align:middle
you know, inflation in wages
and those sorts of things?

30:01.016 --> 30:03.826 align:middle
So how do you sort of,
like, tease out the signal

30:03.826 --> 30:05.396 align:middle
of maximum employment?

30:05.616 --> 30:05.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:05.826 --> 30:08.476 align:middle
Okay. So let me start by
saying that the inflation

30:08.476 --> 30:12.226 align:middle
that we got was not at all the
inflation we were looking for

30:12.226 --> 30:13.516 align:middle
or talking about
in the framework.

30:13.516 --> 30:15.866 align:middle
This was-it really was a
completely different thing.

30:15.866 --> 30:18.926 align:middle
It was to do with, you
know, strong monetary policy

30:18.926 --> 30:21.616 align:middle
and fiscal stimulus
into an economy

30:21.616 --> 30:23.406 align:middle
that was recovering rapidly,

30:23.456 --> 30:26.346 align:middle
and in which there were
these supply-side barriers

30:26.346 --> 30:29.806 align:middle
which effectively led to,
you know, in certain parts

30:29.806 --> 30:32.756 align:middle
of the economy, what you might
call a vertical supply curve.

30:32.756 --> 30:36.266 align:middle
So, you know, automobile
purchases are very interest

30:36.266 --> 30:39.326 align:middle
rate-sensitive, and you would
think demand would drive

30:39.326 --> 30:41.726 align:middle
up the quantity of
cars, But it can't,

30:41.726 --> 30:43.156 align:middle
because they don't
have semiconductors.

30:43.156 --> 30:47.696 align:middle
So, so that-it was a very
different kind of inflation.

30:47.696 --> 30:49.236 align:middle
This is not the inflation
we were looking

30:49.236 --> 30:50.596 align:middle
for under our framework at all.

30:50.596 --> 30:51.846 align:middle
It's nothing to do
with our framework.

30:51.846 --> 30:54.706 align:middle
And our-the way we've
approached it is really nothing

30:54.706 --> 30:55.896 align:middle
to do with our framework.

30:55.956 --> 30:57.606 align:middle
But coming to maximum
employment,

30:57.606 --> 30:59.606 align:middle
which is really your question.

30:59.606 --> 31:00.226 align:middle
How do you know?

31:00.226 --> 31:03.156 align:middle
So I think you look at, you
know, prices and quantities.

31:03.366 --> 31:04.866 align:middle
When you-if you want to
look at maximum employment,

31:04.866 --> 31:06.006 align:middle
you look at prices
and quantities,

31:06.006 --> 31:07.786 align:middle
and the main price
you look at is wages.

31:08.056 --> 31:10.146 align:middle
It's one of the things
you look at.

31:10.146 --> 31:14.196 align:middle
And, you know, it's-So
I mentioned a number

31:14.196 --> 31:14.626 align:middle
of other things.

31:14.626 --> 31:17.576 align:middle
You can get to 20, if
you wanted to, easily,

31:17.576 --> 31:20.756 align:middle
but labor force participation,
the unemployment rate,

31:20.756 --> 31:22.606 align:middle
different age groups
of-you know,

31:22.606 --> 31:24.726 align:middle
prime-age labor force
participation, in particular,

31:24.726 --> 31:28.146 align:middle
gets a lot of focus, the
JOLTS data get a lot of focus.

31:28.146 --> 31:32.006 align:middle
And wages, that's, that's
really one of the great signals.

31:32.006 --> 31:33.256 align:middle
The quits rate is another one.

31:34.036 --> 31:37.136 align:middle
The quits rate is really one
of the very best indicators,

31:37.136 --> 31:39.856 align:middle
according to a lot of labor
economists, because people quit

31:39.856 --> 31:41.676 align:middle
because they feel like
they can get a better job.

31:41.676 --> 31:43.326 align:middle
And there's, you
know, record amounts,

31:43.576 --> 31:46.356 align:middle
historically high levels of
that going on-suggesting, again,

31:46.356 --> 31:49.066 align:middle
that you've got a very
tight labor market.

31:49.066 --> 31:53.296 align:middle
So on wages, that's the price
indicator we look at to tell us,

31:53.296 --> 31:54.896 align:middle
along with all the other data,

31:55.036 --> 31:57.616 align:middle
whether we're-whether we
have labor market conditions

31:57.616 --> 31:59.606 align:middle
that are consistent
with maximum employment.

32:00.446 --> 32:02.366 align:middle
And so that's, that's
how we think about it.

32:02.406 --> 32:07.196 align:middle
But the-one of the
complications is that, again,

32:07.196 --> 32:08.876 align:middle
we've got to make
policy in real time.

32:08.876 --> 32:10.246 align:middle
So how do we think about that?

32:10.246 --> 32:12.766 align:middle
Do we-if we think we can
make labor, you know,

32:12.946 --> 32:15.736 align:middle
participation might move
up-if we, let's say we knew

32:15.736 --> 32:17.526 align:middle
that it would start to
move up in two years,

32:17.786 --> 32:18.806 align:middle
would we wait two years

32:18.916 --> 32:21.786 align:middle
when inflation is
running way above target?

32:21.786 --> 32:24.756 align:middle
Probably not, you know, so
you have to make an assessment

32:24.756 --> 32:27.696 align:middle
that what's max-what is the
level of maximum employment

32:27.696 --> 32:31.176 align:middle
that is consistent with price
stability in real time is,

32:31.286 --> 32:32.966 align:middle
is one way to think about it.

32:33.946 --> 32:34.906 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

32:34.906 --> 32:37.446 align:middle
Just to kind of follow
up on that for a second,

32:37.446 --> 32:41.736 align:middle
if you do raise interest
rates next year and, you know,

32:41.886 --> 32:45.146 align:middle
it's-you're not certain whether
you're at maximum employment,

32:45.146 --> 32:48.196 align:middle
are you all going to point-when
you raise interest rates,

32:48.196 --> 32:49.216 align:middle
are you going to point to ways

32:49.216 --> 32:50.756 align:middle
in which the labor market
could still improve?

32:50.756 --> 32:53.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:53.316 --> 32:55.466 align:middle
Yes. I mean, I-absolutely.

32:55.596 --> 33:00.836 align:middle
I, I think-well, whether or not
we-whether or not we say "we're

33:00.836 --> 33:03.836 align:middle
at conditions-labor market
conditions consistent with,

33:04.106 --> 33:08.156 align:middle
with maximum employment" next
year, we would all be open

33:08.156 --> 33:11.626 align:middle
and I think expect, over
time, that the level

33:11.626 --> 33:13.206 align:middle
of maximum employment
that's consistent

33:13.206 --> 33:15.376 align:middle
with price stability
would increase further

33:15.376 --> 33:18.066 align:middle
over time-for example, through
increasing participation.

33:19.176 --> 33:22.986 align:middle
So I-we would certainly-we
would not, in any way,

33:22.986 --> 33:24.396 align:middle
want to foreclose the idea

33:24.396 --> 33:26.196 align:middle
that the labor market
can get even better.

33:26.936 --> 33:28.956 align:middle
But, again, with
inflation as high as it is,

33:28.956 --> 33:30.666 align:middle
we have to make policy
in real time.

33:30.666 --> 33:32.416 align:middle
We've got to make that
assessment in real time.

33:32.416 --> 33:33.046 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:35.776 --> 33:36.446 align:middle
Thank you.

33:36.446 --> 33:38.306 align:middle
We'll go to Olivia
from Bloomberg.

33:38.596 --> 33:39.006 align:middle
OLIVIA ROCKEMAN.

33:40.266 --> 33:41.016 align:middle
Thank you.

33:41.016 --> 33:42.436 align:middle
Good afternoon, Chair
Powell, and thank you

33:42.436 --> 33:43.786 align:middle
for taking our questions.

33:43.786 --> 33:45.846 align:middle
I wanted to follow up on
some of your earlier comments

33:45.846 --> 33:47.226 align:middle
about labor force participation.

33:47.226 --> 33:49.816 align:middle
And I wondered, what do
you think needs to change

33:49.816 --> 33:52.856 align:middle
in the economy to kind of
get a meaningful recovery

33:52.856 --> 33:54.686 align:middle
in labor force participation,

33:54.686 --> 33:56.636 align:middle
and also whether
running the economy hot,

33:56.636 --> 33:58.886 align:middle
like in the last expansion,
is one way of doing that?

33:58.956 --> 33:59.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:00.996 --> 34:03.746 align:middle
Well, the labor market
is, by so many measures,

34:03.746 --> 34:09.456 align:middle
hotter than it ever ran in the
last-in the last expansion,

34:09.606 --> 34:10.366 align:middle
if you think about it.

34:10.366 --> 34:13.346 align:middle
You know, the ratio of job
openings, for example, to,

34:13.576 --> 34:16.266 align:middle
to vacancies is at
all-time highs.

34:16.626 --> 34:20.026 align:middle
Quits, the wages-all those
things are, are even hotter.

34:20.736 --> 34:23.216 align:middle
But what would it take for
labor force participation

34:23.216 --> 34:24.796 align:middle
to move up more?

34:25.356 --> 34:27.026 align:middle
You know, that's-really,
"Why is it low?"

34:27.026 --> 34:27.546 align:middle
is the question.

34:27.546 --> 34:29.856 align:middle
So there, there are
a bunch of answers,

34:29.856 --> 34:33.816 align:middle
and all of them probably have
some, you know, some validity.

34:33.876 --> 34:37.716 align:middle
Part of it will be that
people-for certain people,

34:37.716 --> 34:39.386 align:middle
they don't want to go
back in the labor force

34:39.386 --> 34:41.186 align:middle
because either they're
medically vulnerable

34:41.186 --> 34:45.386 align:middle
or they're not comfortable
going back while COVID is

34:45.386 --> 34:46.226 align:middle
still everywhere.

34:46.226 --> 34:47.756 align:middle
That's one thing.

34:47.756 --> 34:50.556 align:middle
The lack of availability
of childcare made

34:50.556 --> 34:53.096 align:middle
for caretakers is
certainly part of it-not just

34:53.096 --> 34:55.306 align:middle
for children, but
for older people.

34:55.626 --> 34:59.096 align:middle
It has been pointed out by many
that the stock market is high,

34:59.096 --> 35:02.056 align:middle
people's portfolios are
stronger-they may go back

35:02.056 --> 35:04.496 align:middle
to being a one-income rather
than a two-income family.

35:04.806 --> 35:06.666 align:middle
The same thing with
people's houses.

35:07.156 --> 35:10.526 align:middle
People buy-they have a mortgage,
you know, with leverage,

35:11.126 --> 35:13.396 align:middle
and the house price
increases, the equity they have

35:13.396 --> 35:14.366 align:middle
in their home might
have doubled.

35:15.036 --> 35:17.086 align:middle
And they might make-reach
the same conclusion.

35:17.536 --> 35:19.996 align:middle
So they're-and people have
savings on their balance sheet

35:19.996 --> 35:22.916 align:middle
because of forced savings
because they couldn't spend

35:22.916 --> 35:26.336 align:middle
on travel and things like that
and also because of, you know,

35:26.336 --> 35:27.646 align:middle
because of government transfers.

35:27.976 --> 35:30.736 align:middle
So, for all of those
reasons-and it's hard

35:30.736 --> 35:33.866 align:middle
to know exactly the part
each of them plays-we,

35:33.866 --> 35:36.736 align:middle
we have a situation
where we've had a shock

35:36.736 --> 35:39.536 align:middle
to labor force participation
that is not unwinding

35:39.846 --> 35:42.816 align:middle
as quickly as, as
many, as expected.

35:43.456 --> 35:46.936 align:middle
And people-in effect, a good
part of it is voluntary.

35:47.386 --> 35:49.996 align:middle
You know, people-and
this is how they want

35:49.996 --> 35:52.696 align:middle
to maximize their welfare
and that's, that's their,

35:52.696 --> 35:53.976 align:middle
certainly their choice.

35:54.246 --> 35:57.876 align:middle
In other cases, it's something
that will abate very quickly if

35:57.876 --> 36:00.016 align:middle
and when the pandemic
gets under control.

36:00.016 --> 36:02.826 align:middle
And the longer the
pandemic goes on, you know,

36:02.826 --> 36:04.816 align:middle
maybe the less likely
that people will come back

36:04.886 --> 36:07.916 align:middle
because they're-you know, they
get used to their new life

36:07.916 --> 36:09.626 align:middle
and they lose contact
with their old jobs.

36:09.626 --> 36:11.046 align:middle
That's, that's what
the evidence would say.

36:11.046 --> 36:14.206 align:middle
So it's a range of things.

36:14.206 --> 36:16.906 align:middle
It isn't that the
economy lacks stimulus.

36:16.906 --> 36:19.736 align:middle
You know, usually, in
every other expansion,

36:20.146 --> 36:21.396 align:middle
it's that there aren't
enough jobs

36:21.396 --> 36:23.946 align:middle
and people can't find
jobs and, you know,

36:23.946 --> 36:26.446 align:middle
we're stimulating demand and
trying to get demand to come up.

36:26.646 --> 36:27.626 align:middle
That's not the problem here.

36:27.706 --> 36:29.926 align:middle
The problem is a
supply-side problem,

36:29.926 --> 36:32.776 align:middle
which-what it would
take to work it out,

36:32.776 --> 36:33.926 align:middle
I think it's going to be time.

36:33.926 --> 36:37.786 align:middle
And the number-one thing
would, would really be

36:37.786 --> 36:40.076 align:middle
to have the pandemic
get under control.

36:40.486 --> 36:41.976 align:middle
That's what everyone
would really like to see:

36:41.976 --> 36:44.396 align:middle
What does the labor force-what
does the labor market look

36:44.396 --> 36:46.946 align:middle
like in a world without COVID?

36:47.026 --> 36:51.136 align:middle
That would be the thing that
we'd really like to see but,

36:51.226 --> 36:53.346 align:middle
you know, it doesn't look like
that's coming anytime soon.

36:53.346 --> 36:53.736 align:middle
OLIVIA ROCKEMAN.

36:54.036 --> 36:57.936 align:middle
And just to quickly follow up
on that: If some of the reason

36:57.936 --> 37:00.046 align:middle
that labor force participation
isn't back to, you know,

37:00.306 --> 37:05.036 align:middle
February 2020 levels is because
people are voluntarily making

37:05.146 --> 37:06.766 align:middle
life decisions that
are different,

37:07.286 --> 37:08.676 align:middle
does that make you
think we're going to end

37:08.676 --> 37:10.606 align:middle
up at a lower rate overall?

37:11.106 --> 37:12.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:12.376 --> 37:15.346 align:middle
Well, there's a demographic
trend underlying all of this.

37:15.596 --> 37:18.136 align:middle
And we actually got above the
demographic trend at the end

37:18.136 --> 37:19.396 align:middle
of the last expansion.

37:21.466 --> 37:23.996 align:middle
But-so one would
expect over time

37:23.996 --> 37:25.766 align:middle
that labor force
participation would,

37:26.106 --> 37:28.856 align:middle
would move down because
an aging population,

37:28.856 --> 37:31.996 align:middle
the older people are, the lower
their participation rate is.

37:32.426 --> 37:35.776 align:middle
So you would expect that,
that the trend would be lower

37:35.776 --> 37:39.706 align:middle
and that, over time,
participation would move down.

37:40.206 --> 37:44.046 align:middle
The question of how much
we can get back up closer

37:44.046 --> 37:47.646 align:middle
to where we were in February of
2020-and, indeed, for the year

37:47.646 --> 37:49.856 align:middle
or so before that-is a good one.

37:49.856 --> 37:54.146 align:middle
And, I mean, I-What we can do
is try to create the conditions.

37:54.146 --> 37:57.806 align:middle
There's a lot of good for
society when you have a tight

37:58.386 --> 38:01.666 align:middle
but stable labor market,
where people are coming in,

38:02.086 --> 38:03.356 align:middle
they're getting into
the labor force,

38:03.356 --> 38:04.656 align:middle
they're getting paid well.

38:05.356 --> 38:07.906 align:middle
In the labor market
we had before, we had,

38:08.706 --> 38:12.386 align:middle
you know-the biggest wage
increases were going to people

38:12.386 --> 38:13.966 align:middle
at the bottom end
of the wage spectrum

38:13.966 --> 38:15.126 align:middle
for the last couple of years.

38:15.436 --> 38:17.436 align:middle
There were just a lot of
really desirable aspects

38:17.436 --> 38:18.716 align:middle
of a labor market like that.

38:18.886 --> 38:21.326 align:middle
Higher participation
is one of them.

38:21.476 --> 38:22.566 align:middle
And we'd love to get back there.

38:22.566 --> 38:26.036 align:middle
But, again, ultimately, we
have the tools that we have,

38:26.116 --> 38:28.016 align:middle
which are essentially
to stimulate demand

38:28.016 --> 38:30.236 align:middle
and also to control inflation.

38:30.316 --> 38:34.406 align:middle
I mean, really, it might
be-one of the two big threats

38:34.406 --> 38:36.586 align:middle
to getting back to maximum
employment is actually high

38:36.586 --> 38:39.326 align:middle
inflation, because, to
get back to where we were,

38:40.026 --> 38:42.896 align:middle
the evidence grows that it's
going to take some time.

38:43.666 --> 38:46.226 align:middle
And what we need is
another long expansion,

38:46.226 --> 38:47.476 align:middle
like the ones we've been having.

38:47.476 --> 38:49.426 align:middle
Over the last 40 years,
we've had, I think,

38:49.426 --> 38:52.456 align:middle
three of the four longest
in our recorded history,

38:52.576 --> 38:54.526 align:middle
including the last one,
which was the longest

38:54.526 --> 38:55.306 align:middle
in our recorded history.

38:55.666 --> 38:59.446 align:middle
That's what it would really
take to get back to the kind

38:59.536 --> 39:00.976 align:middle
of labor market we'd
like to see.

39:01.026 --> 39:03.426 align:middle
And to have that happen,
we need to make sure

39:03.426 --> 39:05.516 align:middle
that we maintain
price stability.

39:05.946 --> 39:09.006 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:09.006 --> 39:10.956 align:middle
Okay, let's go to
Edward Lawrence at Fox.

39:10.956 --> 39:11.576 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

39:11.876 --> 39:14.196 align:middle
Thanks. Thanks, Michelle,

39:14.196 --> 39:16.966 align:middle
and thanks for taking the
question, Chair Powell.

39:16.966 --> 39:18.656 align:middle
So I was looking at
the consensus data

39:18.656 --> 39:21.306 align:middle
for estimated monthly
changes on sales.

39:21.306 --> 39:24.276 align:middle
And what you're seeing is fewer
people spending at restaurants

39:24.276 --> 39:26.976 align:middle
and drinking places, more people
spending at grocery stores.

39:27.196 --> 39:30.146 align:middle
You see electronic sales down
4.6 percent month over month,

39:30.146 --> 39:33.706 align:middle
also department store sales down
5.4 percent month over month.

39:33.986 --> 39:35.326 align:middle
How concerned or
what is your level

39:35.326 --> 39:38.296 align:middle
of concern the consumer may be
turning away from this economy

39:38.296 --> 39:39.676 align:middle
or pulling back because
of inflation,

39:39.676 --> 39:41.136 align:middle
the virus, or something else?

39:41.136 --> 39:41.516 align:middle
Thank you.

39:42.106 --> 39:42.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:42.436 --> 39:45.906 align:middle
We see consumer expense as
very strong in this quarter.

39:45.906 --> 39:47.416 align:middle
I don't know, Edward,
whether you're talking

39:47.416 --> 39:50.526 align:middle
about more shopping online
versus shopping in the store,

39:50.526 --> 39:54.556 align:middle
but consumer demand is very
strong, incomes are very strong,

39:54.806 --> 39:56.586 align:middle
you know, because people
are going back to work

39:56.586 --> 39:57.926 align:middle
and they're getting
wage increases.

39:57.926 --> 40:00.536 align:middle
Admittedly, some of the wage
increases is being eaten away

40:00.536 --> 40:01.336 align:middle
by inflation.

40:01.336 --> 40:03.586 align:middle
But, nonetheless,
incomes, overall,

40:03.586 --> 40:05.226 align:middle
are going up really
significantly

40:05.226 --> 40:09.606 align:middle
because of increased employment
and spending is-has been strong.

40:10.106 --> 40:12.096 align:middle
There may be something
in the seasonality

40:12.096 --> 40:14.666 align:middle
that this year's holiday
spending may have been

40:14.666 --> 40:15.416 align:middle
pulled forward.

40:16.026 --> 40:19.256 align:middle
And, of course, there may
be effects from, from Delta

40:19.256 --> 40:21.016 align:middle
and there might be going
forward from Omicron.

40:21.016 --> 40:23.756 align:middle
But fundamentally, though, the
consumer is really healthy,

40:24.256 --> 40:26.566 align:middle
and we expect personal
consumption expenditures

40:26.566 --> 40:28.356 align:middle
to be pretty strong
in the fourth quarter.

40:28.786 --> 40:32.236 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:32.236 --> 40:34.326 align:middle
Okay, let's go to
Mike at Bloomberg.

40:35.776 --> 40:36.276 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

40:36.276 --> 40:40.516 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, the median
forecast for inflation

40:40.796 --> 40:45.256 align:middle
in this month's or this
meeting's economic projections

40:45.676 --> 40:49.266 align:middle
has been revised up
significantly for 2021

40:49.266 --> 40:53.926 align:middle
but barely moved
for 2022 and 2023.

40:54.506 --> 40:57.916 align:middle
You've said you expect
inflation to fall significantly.

40:57.916 --> 41:01.536 align:middle
Is that because you're going
to raise interest rates

41:01.616 --> 41:04.306 align:middle
or because the virus
is going to fade

41:04.306 --> 41:05.666 align:middle
and the effects are
going to fade?

41:05.826 --> 41:09.856 align:middle
In other words, is it a
question of when, not if,

41:09.966 --> 41:11.726 align:middle
you raise interest rates?

41:11.826 --> 41:15.236 align:middle
And does it suggest that
maybe your critics are correct

41:15.236 --> 41:17.596 align:middle
and you might be afraid
you're "behind the curve"?

41:17.596 --> 41:18.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:18.406 --> 41:21.396 align:middle
So, actually, I'm
looking at the SEP here,

41:21.396 --> 41:24.376 align:middle
and the median forecasts
for core

41:24.376 --> 41:27.406 align:middle
and headline inflation
did move up by 4/10 each.

41:27.626 --> 41:30.716 align:middle
So that's a significant-you
know, in an SEP,

41:30.716 --> 41:33.516 align:middle
that's a pretty significant
move up.

41:34.286 --> 41:39.566 align:middle
You know, it's, it's based on
both of those things, I suppose.

41:39.666 --> 41:45.206 align:middle
I do think there's a broad
expectation among forecasters,

41:45.206 --> 41:49.566 align:middle
including our own, that the
bottlenecks will alleviate

41:49.696 --> 41:51.266 align:middle
sometime over the
course of this year.

41:51.266 --> 41:54.496 align:middle
If you look at where Blue
Chip forecasts are-which is,

41:54.496 --> 41:56.676 align:middle
you know, a group
of well-resourced,

41:56.676 --> 41:58.446 align:middle
large forecasting operations

41:58.446 --> 42:01.906 align:middle
with a long track record-they'll
show inflation coming back

42:02.316 --> 42:06.176 align:middle
down significantly toward
the back-end of next year.

42:06.656 --> 42:08.836 align:middle
I would say, though, as well

42:08.836 --> 42:10.876 align:middle
that our policy should
begin to have an effect.

42:10.876 --> 42:12.296 align:middle
There will be a lag,
but it should begin

42:12.296 --> 42:13.846 align:middle
to have an effect
on that as well.

42:14.616 --> 42:18.836 align:middle
And, you know, that's
the most likely case.

42:19.466 --> 42:22.316 align:middle
I guess what-the thing I
would want to say, though,

42:22.316 --> 42:26.276 align:middle
is we can't act as though that's
a certainty, and we're not going

42:26.276 --> 42:27.656 align:middle
to act as though
that's a certainty.

42:27.686 --> 42:30.726 align:middle
There's a real risk now,
we believe, I believe,

42:31.426 --> 42:33.526 align:middle
that inflation may
be more persistent

42:33.666 --> 42:36.256 align:middle
and that may be putting
inflation expectations

42:36.686 --> 42:39.066 align:middle
under pressure, and
that the risk

42:39.066 --> 42:42.016 align:middle
of higher inflation becoming
entrenched has increased.

42:42.016 --> 42:43.046 align:middle
It's certainly increased.

42:43.246 --> 42:44.946 align:middle
I don't think it's
high at this moment,

42:45.006 --> 42:45.956 align:middle
but I think it's increased.

42:46.456 --> 42:49.756 align:middle
And I think that's part of the
reason behind our move today.

42:50.236 --> 42:52.296 align:middle
[It] is to put ourselves
in a position to be able

42:52.296 --> 42:53.336 align:middle
to deal with that risk.

42:53.616 --> 42:55.626 align:middle
And I think we are in a
position to deal with that risk.

42:56.226 --> 42:57.466 align:middle
We need to see more data.

42:57.466 --> 43:01.936 align:middle
We need to see how the inflation
data and the-all the data evolve

43:02.426 --> 43:06.236 align:middle
in coming months, but we
are prepared to, you know,

43:06.236 --> 43:07.746 align:middle
to use our tools to make sure

43:07.746 --> 43:11.146 align:middle
that higher inflation
doesn't get entrenched.

43:11.146 --> 43:12.986 align:middle
For one reason, as
I-as I just mentioned,

43:13.386 --> 43:14.796 align:middle
it's one of the two big threats,

43:14.796 --> 43:17.506 align:middle
the other being the pandemic
itself, to getting back

43:17.506 --> 43:18.376 align:middle
to maximum employment.

43:18.796 --> 43:21.456 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:21.456 --> 43:23.096 align:middle
Let's go to Michael Derby
at the Wall Street Journal.

43:23.096 --> 43:23.436 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

43:23.986 --> 43:27.236 align:middle
Yeah. Thanks for
taking my question.

43:27.556 --> 43:30.746 align:middle
So as the Fed shifts
towards an accelerated taper,

43:30.746 --> 43:31.746 align:middle
I wonder what your read is

43:31.746 --> 43:33.716 align:middle
on financial-stability
risks right now.

43:33.716 --> 43:35.696 align:middle
I mean, these periods
can be-you know,

43:35.696 --> 43:38.636 align:middle
it seems like the taper process
has gone fairly smooth so far.

43:38.636 --> 43:42.046 align:middle
But, you know, what do you see
in terms of stability risks?

43:42.046 --> 43:44.146 align:middle
Are there any parts of
the financial sector

43:44.146 --> 43:45.766 align:middle
that concern you right now?

43:46.176 --> 43:49.906 align:middle
And are there any significant
systemic issues that are

43:49.906 --> 43:52.626 align:middle
on your radar, you know, maybe
from the cryptocurrency sector

43:52.626 --> 43:53.636 align:middle
or something like that?

43:54.856 --> 43:55.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:55.296 --> 43:59.086 align:middle
You know, we have-we
have had now for a decade

43:59.086 --> 44:02.486 align:middle
and more a four-part financial
stability framework that we use

44:02.486 --> 44:05.576 align:middle
so we can-we can hold
ourselves to the same kind

44:05.716 --> 44:08.596 align:middle
of framework and, you know,

44:08.596 --> 44:10.826 align:middle
not just treat each
event individually.

44:10.876 --> 44:13.606 align:middle
And there are four key
areas: asset valuations,

44:14.156 --> 44:17.116 align:middle
debt owed by households and
businesses, funding risk,

44:17.416 --> 44:19.166 align:middle
and leverage among
financial institutions.

44:19.166 --> 44:21.556 align:middle
So I would say asset
valuations-I'm going

44:21.676 --> 44:23.106 align:middle
to go really superficially here,

44:23.106 --> 44:25.906 align:middle
but asset valuations are
somewhat elevated, I would say.

44:26.436 --> 44:28.516 align:middle
Debt owed by businesses,
you know,

44:28.706 --> 44:31.466 align:middle
and households-households are
in very strong financial shape.

44:32.046 --> 44:33.806 align:middle
Businesses actually
have a lot of debt,

44:33.856 --> 44:36.486 align:middle
but their default rates
are very, very low.

44:36.966 --> 44:38.826 align:middle
But, nonetheless, it's
something we're watching.

44:38.826 --> 44:43.176 align:middle
Funding risk is, by
and large, low among,

44:43.276 --> 44:45.206 align:middle
among financial institutions.

44:45.466 --> 44:49.976 align:middle
But we do see money market funds
as a vulnerability and, and,

44:50.586 --> 44:54.206 align:middle
you know, would applaud
the SEC's action this week.

44:54.696 --> 44:56.866 align:middle
Leverage among financial
institutions is low,

44:56.866 --> 44:58.226 align:middle
in the sense that
[their] capital is high.

44:58.226 --> 45:02.386 align:middle
So, overall, you know,
financial stability,

45:03.646 --> 45:06.356 align:middle
that's how-I wouldn't make
an overall characteristic,

45:06.356 --> 45:08.026 align:middle
but we break it down
into those pieces.

45:08.426 --> 45:09.646 align:middle
In terms of the things,
you know,

45:09.646 --> 45:14.986 align:middle
that we're-that we're looking
for, looking at, you know,

45:14.986 --> 45:16.686 align:middle
it's the-it's the things
we've already talked about,

45:16.686 --> 45:17.396 align:middle
to some extent.

45:17.396 --> 45:21.236 align:middle
It's, it's the emergence of
a new variant that could,

45:21.236 --> 45:23.956 align:middle
you know, that could lead

45:23.956 --> 45:27.216 align:middle
to significant economic-if
there were to be a variant,

45:27.216 --> 45:29.636 align:middle
for example, [and if] that were
quite resistant to vaccines,

45:29.636 --> 45:31.086 align:middle
it could have another
significant effect

45:31.086 --> 45:31.586 align:middle
on the economy.

45:31.786 --> 45:32.606 align:middle
We don't see that.

45:32.606 --> 45:34.826 align:middle
We don't have any basis for
thinking that the new variant

45:34.826 --> 45:36.416 align:middle
that we have is that one,

45:36.416 --> 45:40.026 align:middle
but it's certainly
one we're looking at.

45:41.586 --> 45:43.966 align:middle
I would say, you
know, cyber risk,

45:44.226 --> 45:48.016 align:middle
the risk of a successful
cyberattack, is, for me,

45:48.286 --> 45:49.916 align:middle
you know, always
the most-you know,

45:49.916 --> 45:53.896 align:middle
one that would be very
difficult to deal with.

45:53.896 --> 45:57.436 align:middle
I think we know how to deal with
bad loans and things like that.

45:57.436 --> 45:59.606 align:middle
I think more-a cyberattack
that were to take

45:59.606 --> 46:01.156 align:middle
down a major financial
institution

46:01.496 --> 46:04.486 align:middle
or financial market utility
would be a really significant

46:04.486 --> 46:07.546 align:middle
financial stability risk that
we haven't actually faced yet.

46:08.306 --> 46:11.806 align:middle
So I could go on with
a list of "horribles,"

46:11.836 --> 46:15.726 align:middle
but I think that's a decent
picture of where I would start.

46:15.726 --> 46:15.996 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

46:17.216 --> 46:19.166 align:middle
How about the cryptocurrency
issues?

46:19.166 --> 46:21.436 align:middle
Anything that worries you
there that's going on in there

46:21.436 --> 46:23.716 align:middle
in terms of-you know,
obviously a lot's happening

46:23.716 --> 46:24.356 align:middle
in that sector.

46:24.356 --> 46:25.996 align:middle
Does that concern you at all?

46:26.876 --> 46:27.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:27.266 --> 46:28.786 align:middle
You know, I think the
concerns there are not

46:28.786 --> 46:31.706 align:middle
so much current
financial-stability concerns.

46:32.256 --> 46:35.276 align:middle
I, of course, would support
the, the views expressed

46:35.326 --> 46:38.836 align:middle
in the President's Working
Group report on stablecoins.

46:38.836 --> 46:42.316 align:middle
Stablecoins can certainly
be a useful, efficient,

46:42.886 --> 46:47.106 align:middle
consumer-serving, part
of the financial system,

46:47.246 --> 46:48.606 align:middle
if they're properly regulated.

46:48.606 --> 46:49.866 align:middle
And, right now, they aren't.

46:50.446 --> 46:53.346 align:middle
And, and they have the potential
to scale [up], particularly

46:53.346 --> 46:55.996 align:middle
if they were to be, you
know, associated with one

46:56.036 --> 46:59.536 align:middle
of the very large tech
networks that exist

46:59.536 --> 47:01.996 align:middle
and you could have
a payment network

47:01.996 --> 47:04.626 align:middle
that was immediately
systemically important-close

47:04.626 --> 47:05.956 align:middle
to immediately systemically
important

47:05.956 --> 47:08.626 align:middle
that didn't have appropriate
regulation and protections.

47:08.626 --> 47:12.176 align:middle
The public relies on the
government-and [on] the Fed,

47:12.176 --> 47:16.396 align:middle
in particular-to make sure
that the payment system is safe

47:16.446 --> 47:19.806 align:middle
and reliable, as well as
the dollar and-you know,

47:19.876 --> 47:22.646 align:middle
to provide a safe and
reliable trusted currency.

47:23.226 --> 47:25.996 align:middle
So-but I do think
those are longer term.

47:25.996 --> 47:30.366 align:middle
In terms of, you know, the
more-the cryptocurrencies,

47:30.366 --> 47:33.136 align:middle
you know, that are
really speculative assets,

47:33.886 --> 47:34.806 align:middle
I don't see them

47:34.806 --> 47:38.916 align:middle
as a financial-stability
concern at the moment.

47:39.596 --> 47:42.866 align:middle
I do think they are risky,
they're not backed by anything,

47:42.936 --> 47:49.716 align:middle
and I think there's big
consumer-issues for consumers

47:49.716 --> 47:51.646 align:middle
who may or may not understand
what they're getting.

47:51.646 --> 47:55.316 align:middle
And there's certainly
developments

47:55.316 --> 47:57.586 align:middle
in the-in the markets
that are worth following,

47:57.586 --> 48:00.116 align:middle
which are really not in our
jurisdiction, but things like,

48:00.116 --> 48:02.116 align:middle
you know, the kind of
leverage that's built into

48:02.116 --> 48:05.306 align:middle
and those sorts of things is
certainly worth, worth watching.

48:05.786 --> 48:07.796 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:07.966 --> 48:10.766 align:middle
Let's go to Nancy
Marshall-Genzer at Marketplace.

48:11.206 --> 48:13.676 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

48:13.756 --> 48:14.016 align:middle
Chair Powell.

48:14.016 --> 48:15.706 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell,
thanks for the question.

48:16.276 --> 48:20.346 align:middle
Going back to inflation, is
the Fed "behind the curve"

48:20.826 --> 48:23.036 align:middle
on getting inflation
under control?

48:24.826 --> 48:25.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:25.916 --> 48:29.806 align:middle
So I would say this.

48:29.806 --> 48:34.056 align:middle
I actually think we are well
positioned to deal with,

48:35.016 --> 48:36.476 align:middle
with what's coming,
with the range

48:36.476 --> 48:38.616 align:middle
of plausible outcomes
that can come.

48:39.556 --> 48:44.016 align:middle
I do. And I think if you
look at how we got here,

48:44.576 --> 48:49.636 align:middle
I do think we've, we've been
adapting to the incoming data,

48:49.816 --> 48:55.526 align:middle
really all the way along, and,
you know, noticing and calling

48:55.526 --> 48:59.456 align:middle
out that, that the-both the
effects and the persistence

48:59.536 --> 49:02.406 align:middle
of inflation of bottlenecks

49:03.156 --> 49:05.416 align:middle
and labor shortages
and things like that.

49:05.676 --> 49:07.876 align:middle
So we've been calling
out the fact

49:07.876 --> 49:10.946 align:middle
that those were becoming longer,
and more persistent, and larger.

49:11.856 --> 49:15.456 align:middle
And now we're in a position
where we're ending our taper

49:15.756 --> 49:20.356 align:middle
within the next-well, by March,
in two meetings, and we'll be

49:20.356 --> 49:22.116 align:middle
in a position to raise
interest rates as

49:22.116 --> 49:23.656 align:middle
and when we think
it's appropriate.

49:23.656 --> 49:26.026 align:middle
And we will, if that-to the
extent that's appropriate.

49:26.026 --> 49:27.826 align:middle
At the same time, we're going

49:27.826 --> 49:29.306 align:middle
to be seeing a few
more months of data.

49:30.026 --> 49:32.226 align:middle
I don't actually think
we're out of position now.

49:32.226 --> 49:34.616 align:middle
I think this was an
important move for us to make.

49:35.066 --> 49:38.306 align:middle
I think that the data
that we got toward the end

49:38.306 --> 49:42.586 align:middle
of the fall was a-was
a really strong signal

49:43.266 --> 49:47.826 align:middle
that inflation is more
persistent and higher-and

49:47.826 --> 49:50.706 align:middle
that the risk of it remaining
higher for longer has grown.

49:51.246 --> 49:52.686 align:middle
And I think we're
reacting to that now.

49:52.686 --> 49:54.326 align:middle
And we'll continue
to adapt our policy.

49:54.326 --> 49:57.516 align:middle
So I wouldn't look at it that
we're "behind the curve."

49:57.516 --> 49:59.796 align:middle
I would look at it that we're
actually in position now

49:59.836 --> 50:03.646 align:middle
to take the steps that we'll
need to take, you know,

50:03.646 --> 50:07.036 align:middle
in a thoughtful manner to
address all of the issues,

50:07.336 --> 50:09.346 align:middle
including that of, of
too-high inflation.

50:09.826 --> 50:11.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

50:11.526 --> 50:13.836 align:middle
Let's go to Evan at Market News.

50:14.496 --> 50:17.156 align:middle
EVAN RYSER.

50:17.246 --> 50:17.896 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

50:18.026 --> 50:18.496 align:middle
Thank you.

50:18.496 --> 50:23.036 align:middle
I was wondering if we should
still be seeing the taper

50:23.316 --> 50:25.246 align:middle
and interest rate
hikes as separate.

50:25.876 --> 50:28.276 align:middle
And, secondly, in
the last cycle,

50:28.276 --> 50:30.066 align:middle
the Fed started shrinking
the balance sheet

50:30.316 --> 50:31.626 align:middle
when short-term interest
rates were

50:31.626 --> 50:34.636 align:middle
about 1 to 1¼ percent range.

50:35.046 --> 50:37.256 align:middle
Do you think the
FOMC might be able

50:37.256 --> 50:40.646 align:middle
to potentially start running off
assets before that this time?

50:40.646 --> 50:43.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:44.076 --> 50:44.896 align:middle
Are they separate?

50:44.896 --> 50:48.296 align:middle
So our interest rate-I mean,
they are separate tools.

50:48.886 --> 50:51.946 align:middle
So the-you know, the, the asset
purchases are a separate tool

50:51.946 --> 50:52.826 align:middle
from interest rates.

50:52.976 --> 50:56.246 align:middle
Stopping asset purchases does
not remove accommodation.

50:56.246 --> 50:58.226 align:middle
It just stops adding
further accommodation.

50:58.226 --> 51:01.526 align:middle
Whereas raising interest rates
starts to remove accommodation

51:01.526 --> 51:04.986 align:middle
from what is a highly
accommodative stance.

51:05.306 --> 51:09.496 align:middle
You know, the extent to which
they'll be separated in time is,

51:10.196 --> 51:12.616 align:middle
is something we haven't
really discussed

51:12.616 --> 51:13.506 align:middle
at the Committee [meetings] yet.

51:13.506 --> 51:15.886 align:middle
We will be discussing that,
obviously, in coming meetings.

51:16.466 --> 51:19.496 align:middle
I don't think that,
that-the last cycle,

51:19.496 --> 51:21.686 align:middle
there was quite a
long separation before

51:22.316 --> 51:22.986 align:middle
interest rates.

51:22.986 --> 51:25.196 align:middle
I don't think that's at
all likely in this cycle.

51:25.196 --> 51:27.506 align:middle
We're in a very, very different
place with high inflation,

51:27.766 --> 51:29.716 align:middle
strong growth, a
really strong economy.

51:30.316 --> 51:33.046 align:middle
You know, that-as I
mentioned, the SEP medians are

51:33.046 --> 51:34.756 align:middle
for 4 percent growth next year,

51:34.756 --> 51:38.236 align:middle
3½ percent-3½ percent
unemployment at the end

51:38.236 --> 51:41.526 align:middle
of the year, and the, the-you
know, headline inflation

51:41.526 --> 51:44.126 align:middle
of 2.6 percent next
year, core at 2.7.

51:44.476 --> 51:47.896 align:middle
So this is a strong economy, one
in which it's appropriate for,

51:48.396 --> 51:49.546 align:middle
for interest rate hikes.

51:49.586 --> 51:51.136 align:middle
So they're separate,
I would say.

51:51.616 --> 51:53.306 align:middle
Your-sorry, your
second question was?

51:53.306 --> 51:53.826 align:middle
EVAN RYSER.

51:54.256 --> 51:56.766 align:middle
My second question
was about runoff.

51:56.846 --> 51:57.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:57.146 --> 51:58.906 align:middle
Runoff. EVAN RYSER.

51:58.906 --> 51:59.696 align:middle
In the last cycle.

51:59.696 --> 52:00.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:00.586 --> 52:03.386 align:middle
So, you know, with
the balance sheet,

52:03.386 --> 52:04.746 align:middle
we did have a balance
sheet discussion,

52:04.746 --> 52:07.526 align:middle
sort of a prelim-a
first, first discussion

52:07.526 --> 52:10.706 align:middle
of balance sheet issues
today, [balance] sheet issues,

52:10.706 --> 52:12.236 align:middle
at our meeting this week.

52:12.786 --> 52:14.516 align:middle
We'll have another at the
next meeting and another

52:14.516 --> 52:16.406 align:middle
at the meeting after
that I suspect.

52:16.406 --> 52:18.766 align:middle
These are interesting
issues to discuss.

52:18.806 --> 52:20.236 align:middle
[We] didn't make
any decisions today.

52:20.746 --> 52:25.136 align:middle
We looked back at what
happened in the last cycle,

52:25.426 --> 52:27.546 align:middle
and people thought that was
interesting and informative.

52:27.716 --> 52:31.096 align:middle
And-but to one degree
or another, people noted

52:31.096 --> 52:32.846 align:middle
that this is just a
different situation,

52:32.846 --> 52:35.426 align:middle
and those differences should
inform the decisions we make

52:35.426 --> 52:36.636 align:middle
about the balance
sheet this time.

52:37.146 --> 52:39.246 align:middle
So [we] haven't made any
decisions at all about,

52:39.786 --> 52:41.226 align:middle
about when runoff would start,

52:41.856 --> 52:44.956 align:middle
but we're-we'll be
continuing to-you know,

52:44.956 --> 52:49.156 align:middle
in relation to when
either liftoff happens

52:49.156 --> 52:50.216 align:middle
or the end of the taper.

52:50.416 --> 52:52.506 align:middle
But those are exactly the
decisions we'll be turning

52:52.506 --> 52:53.616 align:middle
to in coming meetings.

52:53.786 --> 52:54.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

52:56.866 --> 52:57.536 align:middle
Thank you.

52:57.536 --> 52:59.286 align:middle
We'll go to Scott
Horsley at NPR.

52:59.836 --> 53:02.046 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

53:02.266 --> 53:03.216 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

53:03.706 --> 53:06.336 align:middle
I think you said a few
minutes ago that the inflation

53:06.336 --> 53:09.176 align:middle
that we got during the
pandemic was not the inflation

53:09.176 --> 53:12.546 align:middle
that we-that you anticipated
when you crafted the framework.

53:12.976 --> 53:16.726 align:middle
And you described it as a
collision of a lot of monetary

53:16.726 --> 53:20.026 align:middle
and fiscal stimulus with
these supply-side hiccups.

53:20.086 --> 53:25.146 align:middle
Does that mean the
stimulus was mistaken,

53:25.206 --> 53:29.336 align:middle
or is the inflation just a
consequence of-that we have

53:29.336 --> 53:31.026 align:middle
to put up with because of that?

53:31.026 --> 53:31.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

53:31.646 --> 53:34.076 align:middle
No, I'm not expressing
any judgment about,

53:34.176 --> 53:36.596 align:middle
about the stimulus
in, in that comment.

53:36.596 --> 53:41.976 align:middle
What I'm saying is, there's
a-there's a sense among some

53:41.976 --> 53:44.646 align:middle
that "you wanted inflation,
this is what you wanted,

53:44.646 --> 53:45.596 align:middle
how do you like it?," you know?

53:45.596 --> 53:47.486 align:middle
And the truth is, this
is not the inflation

53:47.486 --> 53:48.946 align:middle
that we were-what
we were talking

53:48.946 --> 53:52.096 align:middle
about in the-in the framework
was inflation that comes

53:52.096 --> 53:54.016 align:middle
from a tight labor
market, right?

53:54.016 --> 53:58.146 align:middle
So we had 3½ percent
unemployment for a period,

53:58.376 --> 54:00.446 align:middle
and we had inflation that
was just barely getting

54:00.446 --> 54:01.226 align:middle
to 2 percent.

54:01.796 --> 54:05.816 align:middle
And I-you know, I think, in
that setting, our thinking was,

54:06.086 --> 54:08.116 align:middle
we can afford to
wait to raise rates

54:08.606 --> 54:10.396 align:middle
until we see actual
inflation rather

54:10.396 --> 54:12.046 align:middle
than [acting] preemptive[ly]
because, you know,

54:12.286 --> 54:16.496 align:middle
no one had seen what 3½
percent unemployment would look

54:16.496 --> 54:18.806 align:middle
like-with high labor force
participation, by the way.

54:19.186 --> 54:21.246 align:middle
No one had seen what it
would look-really look

54:21.246 --> 54:23.016 align:middle
like for an extended
period, for decades.

54:23.536 --> 54:26.056 align:middle
And we didn't know what the
inflationary implications were.

54:26.316 --> 54:30.746 align:middle
It turned out there was barely
2 percent inflation-and no sense

54:30.796 --> 54:33.406 align:middle
that it was gaining momentum
and that kind of thing.

54:33.406 --> 54:35.186 align:middle
So we incorporated that
into our framework.

54:35.716 --> 54:37.156 align:middle
This is something
completely different.

54:37.156 --> 54:40.206 align:middle
You know, that's a situation
where you had a very,

54:40.206 --> 54:43.676 align:middle
very high level of
employment and low inflation.

54:44.066 --> 54:45.446 align:middle
This is literally the opposite.

54:45.676 --> 54:47.286 align:middle
Or, you know, it has
been the opposite,

54:47.286 --> 54:49.946 align:middle
where we have very high
inflation and we've had it

54:50.436 --> 54:52.806 align:middle
since the labor market
was in terrible shape.

54:52.806 --> 54:57.256 align:middle
It had nothing-so far, this
inflation has really nothing

54:57.256 --> 54:59.646 align:middle
to do with tightness
in the labor market.

54:59.646 --> 55:02.676 align:middle
It does have to do with,
with strong demand,

55:02.676 --> 55:04.576 align:middle
and strong demand was
supported by the Fed,

55:04.576 --> 55:05.956 align:middle
it was supported by Congress.

55:06.376 --> 55:09.336 align:middle
I'm not making any judgments
on Congress, it's not my job.

55:09.336 --> 55:12.276 align:middle
But I will just say, we're
coming out of a, you know,

55:12.276 --> 55:14.536 align:middle
what we certainly hope will
be a once in a lifetime,

55:14.536 --> 55:18.016 align:middle
certainly historic-the
first really global modern

55:18.016 --> 55:20.406 align:middle
pandemic-which looked
at the beginning

55:20.406 --> 55:22.566 align:middle
like it might cause
a global depression.

55:23.226 --> 55:25.616 align:middle
And, you know, so we threw a lot
of support at it [the economy].

55:25.746 --> 55:28.026 align:middle
And what's coming out
now is, is, you know,

55:28.496 --> 55:31.256 align:middle
really strong growth, really
strong demand, high incomes,

55:31.256 --> 55:33.086 align:middle
and all that kind of thing.

55:33.086 --> 55:34.636 align:middle
You know, people will judge

55:34.636 --> 55:38.076 align:middle
in 25 years whether we-whether
we overdid it or not, but,

55:38.076 --> 55:40.026 align:middle
you know, the reality
is, we are where we are.

55:40.476 --> 55:43.226 align:middle
And, you know, we think our
policy is, is the right one

55:43.226 --> 55:44.776 align:middle
for the situation that we're in.

55:44.776 --> 55:45.406 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

55:47.576 --> 55:48.186 align:middle
Thank you.

55:48.186 --> 55:49.186 align:middle
Let's go to Brian Cheung.

55:50.826 --> 55:51.296 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

55:51.426 --> 55:52.136 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

55:52.136 --> 55:54.266 align:middle
Wanted to ask about
the bond markets.

55:54.396 --> 55:58.116 align:middle
When you see the 10-year
at 146 basis points,

55:58.116 --> 56:01.166 align:middle
do you have any sort of
concerns about an environment

56:01.166 --> 56:03.636 align:middle
by which you might be
hiking interest rates into?

56:03.636 --> 56:06.056 align:middle
Would you prefer the curve
be a little bit steeper?

56:06.056 --> 56:08.266 align:middle
What are you gleaning from
the bond market actions

56:08.266 --> 56:09.236 align:middle
over the last six weeks?

56:09.236 --> 56:09.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

56:09.456 --> 56:13.626 align:middle
So I, I think the short-end
actions are easy to understand,

56:13.626 --> 56:15.276 align:middle
which is, you know, they're
basically very [monetary]

56:15.276 --> 56:18.126 align:middle
policy-sensitive rates at the
short end, and it makes sense

56:18.126 --> 56:21.376 align:middle
that it's reacting to changes
in expectations for policy.

56:21.706 --> 56:24.806 align:middle
I think a lot of things go into
the, you know, the long rates.

56:25.236 --> 56:27.706 align:middle
And the place I would
start is just look

56:27.706 --> 56:30.076 align:middle
at global sovereign
yields around the world.

56:30.576 --> 56:34.406 align:middle
Look at JGBs, look at bunds,
and they're so much lower.

56:34.406 --> 56:39.066 align:middle
You can get-you can get, you
know, a much higher yield

56:39.236 --> 56:44.406 align:middle
on U.S. Treasuries by buying
USTs rather than bunds

56:44.406 --> 56:47.696 align:middle
and you can-you can hedge, hedge
the currency risk back into yen,

56:47.866 --> 56:51.126 align:middle
back into euros and
still be way ahead.

56:51.126 --> 56:54.106 align:middle
So, in a way, it's not
surprising that there's a lot

56:54.106 --> 56:57.096 align:middle
of demand for, for U.S.
sovereigns in a world-you know,

56:57.096 --> 56:59.916 align:middle
in a risk-free world
where, where there's

56:59.916 --> 57:03.956 align:middle
so much-they're yielding so much
more than bunds are, than JGBs.

57:04.056 --> 57:07.066 align:middle
So that's, that's
a big part of it.

57:07.066 --> 57:11.456 align:middle
I also think, you know, there,
there may be some assessment

57:11.456 --> 57:13.146 align:middle
in there of what
the neutral rate is

57:13.146 --> 57:14.416 align:middle
or what the terminal rate is.

57:14.906 --> 57:16.136 align:middle
I don't know about that.

57:16.136 --> 57:18.576 align:middle
But-and I would just
say that we, you know,

57:18.576 --> 57:21.716 align:middle
we write down our own estimates
of the-of the terminal rate

57:21.716 --> 57:22.976 align:middle
or the neutral rate of interest.

57:23.466 --> 57:24.816 align:middle
Those are highly uncertain.

57:24.816 --> 57:27.806 align:middle
And, you know, we'll make policy
based on what we're seeing

57:28.176 --> 57:30.276 align:middle
in the economy rather than based

57:30.276 --> 57:33.696 align:middle
on what a neutral-what a model
might say the neutral rate is.

57:33.696 --> 57:37.706 align:middle
We, we all had the experience
of the last cycle, where we all,

57:37.706 --> 57:38.726 align:middle
through that cycle, were trying

57:38.726 --> 57:40.206 align:middle
to estimate what the
neutral rate was,

57:40.206 --> 57:42.376 align:middle
and it turned out-you know,
I think we learned a lot

57:42.376 --> 57:43.766 align:middle
from seeing what happens.

57:43.766 --> 57:46.676 align:middle
We ended up cutting rates
three times after raising them

57:46.676 --> 57:49.216 align:middle
to 2¼ to 2½ percent.

57:49.646 --> 57:52.376 align:middle
So I'm not troubled by where
the long bond [rate] is.

57:53.146 --> 57:55.816 align:middle
You know, I see that it's low.

57:57.016 --> 58:00.066 align:middle
You know, I mean,
we're really focused

58:00.066 --> 58:02.656 align:middle
on broader financial
conditions, [and] we're focused

58:02.656 --> 58:04.626 align:middle
on maximum employment
and price stability.

58:04.626 --> 58:05.266 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

58:08.166 --> 58:08.906 align:middle
Thank you.

58:08.906 --> 58:10.726 align:middle
For the last question,
we'll go to Greg Robb.

58:10.976 --> 58:11.416 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

58:12.306 --> 58:13.556 align:middle
Thank you very much.

58:14.636 --> 58:16.466 align:middle
Chair Powell, I was
wondering if-I wanted

58:16.956 --> 58:19.796 align:middle
to give you the opportunity
to talk about something

58:19.796 --> 58:22.366 align:middle
that I've heard that's been
discussed-that, you know,

58:22.366 --> 58:26.276 align:middle
your pivot towards
a tighter policy,

58:26.276 --> 58:29.536 align:middle
hawkish policy stance had
something to do with the timing

58:29.536 --> 58:31.566 align:middle
of your renomination
by the President.

58:32.626 --> 58:33.116 align:middle
Thank you.

58:34.486 --> 58:35.006 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

58:35.006 --> 58:37.016 align:middle
Sure, I'd be happy
to talk about that.

58:37.226 --> 58:42.306 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, we got the
ECI reading just before the

58:42.306 --> 58:44.036 align:middle
November meeting.

58:44.686 --> 58:49.646 align:middle
We got the labor market report
two days after the meeting.

58:49.646 --> 58:52.186 align:middle
And then, one week after that,
I think on the 12th of November,

58:52.186 --> 58:53.586 align:middle
we got the CPI reading.

58:53.586 --> 58:57.066 align:middle
It was really the CPI reading,
in concert with those two,

58:57.606 --> 59:01.136 align:middle
and I, I just came to the view
over that weekend that we needed

59:01.136 --> 59:02.896 align:middle
to speed up the taper.

59:03.136 --> 59:04.156 align:middle
And we started working on that.

59:04.766 --> 59:06.776 align:middle
That's a full 10 days

59:06.776 --> 59:10.826 align:middle
or so before the President made
a decision of renominating me.

59:10.826 --> 59:14.656 align:middle
So, honestly, it had-it had
nothing to do with that at all.

59:14.846 --> 59:17.886 align:middle
And I just thought this
is what we got to do.

59:18.226 --> 59:21.836 align:middle
My colleagues were out talking
about the-a faster taper.

59:22.276 --> 59:23.826 align:middle
And that doesn't
happen by accident.

59:24.296 --> 59:26.516 align:middle
They were out talking about
a faster taper before the

59:26.516 --> 59:27.596 align:middle
President made his decision.

59:27.596 --> 59:31.366 align:middle
So it's a decision that
effectively was in-more

59:31.366 --> 59:33.466 align:middle
than in train and,
you know, to the point

59:33.466 --> 59:36.206 align:middle
where people were
talking about it publicly.

59:36.266 --> 59:38.026 align:middle
So that's what happened.

59:38.026 --> 59:40.786 align:middle
And it had absolutely nothing
to do with it whatsoever.

59:40.786 --> 59:43.556 align:middle
We're always going to just
do what we think is the right

59:43.556 --> 59:46.156 align:middle
thing, and I certainly will
always just do what I think the

59:46.156 --> 59:48.336 align:middle
right thing is for the economy

59:48.336 --> 59:49.616 align:middle
and for the people
that we serve.

59:50.176 --> 59:51.176 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

59:51.366 --> 59:52.606 align:middle
Just a quick follow-up.

59:52.606 --> 59:55.656 align:middle
I guess that some people
are saying that it was the,

59:55.656 --> 59:59.076 align:middle
you know, the stimulus
in March that was sort

59:59.076 --> 01:00:02.166 align:middle
of over-we didn't really
need all that stimulus,

01:00:02.166 --> 01:00:03.726 align:middle
and that there was
talk even then

01:00:03.726 --> 01:00:04.966 align:middle
that that would be a mistake

01:00:04.966 --> 01:00:06.496 align:middle
and it would lead
to higher inflation.

01:00:07.166 --> 01:00:09.566 align:middle
And perhaps that you
didn't push back as much

01:00:09.566 --> 01:00:11.206 align:middle
as you might have otherwise.

01:00:11.206 --> 01:00:11.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

01:00:11.846 --> 01:00:13.806 align:middle
I didn't push back at all.

01:00:14.056 --> 01:00:16.716 align:middle
And the reason I didn't-and
there was no-there was lots

01:00:16.716 --> 01:00:18.296 align:middle
of talk about that,
but not from the Fed,

01:00:18.376 --> 01:00:19.846 align:middle
because that is not our job.

01:00:20.516 --> 01:00:24.166 align:middle
We are not the CBO, and
we're not elected by anybody.

01:00:24.166 --> 01:00:28.546 align:middle
So we take fiscal policy as
it arrives at our front door,

01:00:28.586 --> 01:00:30.626 align:middle
and we don't comment-you know,

01:00:31.076 --> 01:00:33.836 align:middle
we make our own assessments
inside the Fed,

01:00:33.836 --> 01:00:35.176 align:middle
but it's really not our role.

01:00:35.176 --> 01:00:36.916 align:middle
And I think, very
important, that we stay out of

01:00:36.916 --> 01:00:38.916 align:middle
that business, no matter who's

01:00:38.916 --> 01:00:40.296 align:middle
in the White House,
who's in Congress.

01:00:40.296 --> 01:00:42.186 align:middle
It's just-it's just not our job

